    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/event-catering-assistant-xm50897": {
      "title": "Event Catering Assistant",
      "description": "Event Catering Assistant | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Event Catering Assistant Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Event Catering Assistant We are seeking an enthusiastic and customer-focused Events Assistant to join our Hospitality Team and support the delivery of a wide range of events across our venues. This is an exciting opportunity for an individual who enjoys working in a fast-paced environment and takes pride in delivering exceptional service and memorable experiences for guests. The successful candidate will play a key role in the planning, preparation, and delivery of hospitality and catered events, ranging from meetings and conferences to receptions, dinners, and special occasions. Responsibilities will include setting up event spaces, serving food and beverages, liaising with clients and colleagues, and ensuring that all events are delivered to the highest standards of presentation and customer service. We are looking for an organised and adaptable individual with excellent communication skills and a positive, can-do attitude. The ability to work effectively as part of a team, pay close attention to detail, and remain professional under pressure is essential. Previous experience in hospitality, catering, or events would be advantageous; however, a genuine passion for customer service and a willingness to learn are equally important.\nThis role offers the opportunity to be part of a dynamic and supportive hospitality team, helping to deliver outstanding events within a vibrant academic environment. Please be aware that a Full UK Driving Licence is essential for this role (a full EU Driving Licence is acceptable on appointment, but the successful applicant must transfer to a UK Licence prior to confirming a start date). Benefits of working at the University of Cambridge include: Competitive pay with automatic annual cost of living increases and service-related progression. Generous annual leave entitlement, helping you maintain a healthy work-life balance. Family-friendly policies, including enhanced maternity, adoption, and shared parental leave, plus access to workplace nurseries. Excellent pension scheme with a generous employer contribution through auto-enrolment. Travel and shopping discounts at over 2,000 local and national retailers. Relocation support for eligible new starters. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you have any questions about this vacancy or the application process, please email Estates Division on EDR@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference XM50897 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information - Event Catering Assistant Key information Department/location Estates Division (Business Services) Salary £25,249-£27,319 Reference XM50897 Category Assistant staff Date published 3 September 2026 Closing date 17 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/events-and-communications-coordinator-fixed-term-vm50953": {
      "title": "Events and Communications Coordinator (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Events and Communications Coordinator (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Events and Communications Coordinator (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Events and Communications Coordinator (Fixed Term) The Centre for Geopolitics is seeking an enthusiastic, highly organised and proactive Events & Communications Coordinator to join our growing team. This role is offered full-time with some occasional need for flexibility to attend evening events and will be fixed term for 12 months due to limited funding. Based in Fitzwilliam House, opposite the Fitzwilliam Museum in central Cambridge, the role is primarily office-based, although occasional remote working may be agreed where appropriate, generally a maximum of one day a week. This is an exciting opportunity to play a central role in delivering a dynamic programme of internationally recognised events and communications activities. Working with leading academics, policymakers and practitioners, you will help ensure the smooth delivery and promotion of a wide range of initiatives that advance the Centre's work on some of the most pressing geopolitical challenges facing the world today. The Centre delivers around 100 events each year, both online and in person, ranging from small closed-door roundtables to major public conferences and symposia attracting audiences of over 150 participants (or occasionally over 500). As Events & Communications Coordinator, you will be at the heart of this programme, managing event logistics from planning to delivery, while helping to maximise the impact and reach of our activities through effective communications, and keeping a clear overview of the overlapping timelines of each event. This is a varied and fast-paced role that requires excellent organisational skills, sound judgement, and the ability to manage multiple priorities simultaneously. You will work closely with academics and programme leaders across the Centre, ensuring events are delivered to a high professional standard and that key deadlines are met. Alongside event management responsibilities, you will contribute to the promotion of the Centre's work through our website, social media channels and other communications platforms. We welcome applicants who can bring fresh ideas for reaching and engaging new audiences. Reporting to the Centre Coordinator, the postholder will work as part of a collaborative team. You will be a confident and highly organised administrator with excellent attention to detail and strong interpersonal skills. You will enjoy managing a busy and varied workload, taking initiative, and working independently when required. Experience of organising multiple, largescale, public-facing events and supporting communications activities through a variety of platforms is essential, as are outstanding verbal and written communication skills, and a truly unflappable nature. Most importantly, you will be a reliable and adaptable team player who thrives in a dynamic environment and enjoys helping others deliver ambitious projects successfully. Informal enquiries about the role are welcomed and should be directed to Charis Whitcombe on cw827@cam.ac.uk . Questions on the application process should be directed to the School's HR Team on sahhr@admin.cam.ac.uk . The closing date for applications is midnight (BST) on Sunday 27 September 2026. Interviews are planned for the week commencing 04 October 2026, subject to change. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please quote reference VM50953 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information Key information Department/location Centre for Research in Arts, Social Sciences and Humanities Salary £31,236-£35,608 Reference VM50953 Category Assistant staff Date published 3 September 2026 Closing date 27 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/executive-assistant-cambridge-conservation-initiative-part-time-ns50987": {
      "title": "Executive Assistant, Cambridge Conservation Initiative (Part- Time)",
      "description": "Executive Assistant, Cambridge Conservation Initiative (Part- Time) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Executive Assistant, Cambridge Conservation Initiative (Part- Time) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Executive Assistant, Cambridge Conservation Initiative (Part- Time) The Cambridge Conservation Initiative (CCI) is seeking an exceptional Executive Assistant to provide high-level support to the Executive Director and contribute to the smooth running of one of the world's leading conservation partnerships.\nCCI brings together the University of Cambridge and internationally renowned conservation organisations to tackle the global biodiversity crisis through research, education, policy and practice. Based in the iconic David Attenborough Building, CCI is a dynamic and collaborative community working at the forefront of conservation impact. This is an exciting opportunity for an experienced and highly organised professional who thrives in a fast-paced environment and enjoys working with senior leaders, academics, external partners and international stakeholders. As Executive Assistant to the Executive Director, you will manage a complex and demanding schedule, coordinate communications and correspondence, arrange international travel, and ensure the effective administration of meetings and activities. You will play a key role in maximising the Director's time and supporting strategic priorities across the partnership. Beyond executive support, you will provide administrative and logistical assistance across the CCI team. You will coordinate meeting logistics, prepare documentation, support governance activities, take minutes when required and ensure efficient follow-up of actions. You will also contribute to the effective operation of the Executive Director's Office, helping to maintain office systems, coordinate team activities, support internal communications and create a welcoming and professional environment for visitors and colleagues alike. The successful candidate will have substantial experience providing senior administrative support, ideally at Director level, together with excellent organisational, communication and interpersonal skills. You will be able to manage competing priorities, exercise sound judgement, maintain confidentiality and build effective relationships with a wide range of stakeholders. A proactive approach, strong attention to detail and the confidence to work independently are essential. An interest in conservation or environmental issues would be advantageous. In return, we offer the opportunity to work in a unique partnership environment, supporting influential leaders and contributing to work that addresses some of the most pressing environmental challenges facing the world today. If you are an experienced Executive Assistant looking for a varied and rewarding role within a globally respected institution, we would be delighted to hear from you. This is a part-time role, working 4 days per week. We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for part-time working or other flexible working arrangements. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal queries may be directed to hrsupport@jbs.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference NS50987 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information FPs Key information Department/location Judge Business School Salary £31,236-£35,608 pro rata Reference NS50987 Category Assistant staff Date published 8 September 2026 Closing date 22 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/executive-manager-industrial-engagement-and-ittp-manager-pu51018": {
      "title": "Executive Manager",
      "description": "Executive Manager - Industrial Engagement and ITTP Manager | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Executive Manager - Industrial Engagement and ITTP Manager Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Executive Manager - Industrial Engagement and ITTP Manager The Medical Research Council Toxicology Unit is an internationally renowned institution which delivers field-changing mechanistic insights into toxicology & disease and in 2027 will become the centre for Safety Science, within the Department of Biochemistry in Cambridge. We are looking to appoint an Executive Manager (EM) who will be responsible for the strategic oversight of all activities linked to industry engagement, maintaining existing partnerships with external stakeholders and seeking out and developing new ones. \nIn particular, the EM will be responsible for the project management of the MRC Prosperity Partnership including the strategic alignment of the award's impacts with other Prosperity Partnership grant holders.  In this role the EM will independently lead the coordination of the multi-partner (academia and industry) MRC Prosperity Partnership to ensure effective delivery across all work packages and ensure strategic alignment with other award holders. The Executive Manager will oversee additional Industrial Liaison activities and provide strategic oversight during the transition to the centre for Safety Science.\nThe EM will also manage the Integrative Toxicology Training Partnership (ITTP) PhD training programme (managed by the MRC Toxicology Unit on behalf of the MRC) and contribute to the UK-wide strategy for toxicology training. Overall, the Executive Manager will help to support industry engagement to maximise the impact of cutting-edge research. The successful candidate must hold a PhD in biological sciences and have a general understanding of toxicology and key issues associated with medicines safety and drug development. You will have links with a variety of stakeholders and experience of the UK HE Sector. Strong interpersonal skills are required together with project management experience. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available until 30 August 2030 in the first instance. We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for part-time working or other flexible working arrangements. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Further information about the Toxicology Unit can be found on our website: https://www.mrc-tox.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference PU51018 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information PU51018 Executive Manager Key information Department/location MRC Toxicology Unit Salary £42,254-£56,535 Reference PU51018 Category Academic-related Date published 9 September 2026 Closing date 30 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/food-service-assistants-xm50899": {
      "title": "Food Service Assistants",
      "description": "Food Service Assistants | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Food Service Assistants Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Food Service Assistants Role Introduction/ About the Role: The Food Service Assistant role plays an important role in delivering friendly, efficient, and high quality service across cafés, catering outlets, and events throughout the University Estate. Whether preparing great barista coffee, serving food, or supporting hospitality for meetings and functions, this role helps create a welcoming experience for students, staff, and visitors. Working as part of a flexible and customer focused team, the Food Service Assistant contributes to smooth day to day operations and the successful delivery of events of all sizes. Key Responsibilities: Prepare and serve hot and cold drinks, including barista coffees, to a consistently high standard. Carry out basic food production tasks Serve food and snacks, ensuring excellent presentation and a friendly, efficient customer experience. Support University events, assisting with hospitality service, food preparation, and setup/clear down across multiple sites. Maintain high standards of cleanliness, ensuring service areas, counters, and equipment are clean, organised, and compliant with hygiene regulations. Assist with stock rotation and replenishment, keeping displays well presented and reporting low stock or issues to supervisors. Handle payments and operate tills, following cash handling procedures accurately. Work collaboratively with catering and events teams, helping to deliver smooth, coordinated service. Follow food safety, allergen, and health & safety procedures, ensuring a safe environment for customers and colleagues. Support the movement of supplies and equipment between outlets and event locations when required. Provide excellent customer service, responding to queries and ensuring all guests feel welcomed and supported. About You You're friendly, reliable, and take pride in creating a positive experience for every guest. You enjoy working as part of a team and bring a helpful, can do attitude to each shift. You're comfortable in a busy environment, staying organised and focused even when things get lively. You pay attention to detail, follow food safety standards, and understand the importance of cleanliness and presentation. Whether you're serving customers, preparing simple food items, or keeping the service area tidy, you approach every task with care and enthusiasm. Above all, you enjoy working with people and contributing to a welcoming, efficient, and well run service. Applications will be reviewed regularly and we reserve the right to close this vacancy early if we receive sufficient applications for the role. Therefore, if you are interested, please submit your application as early as possible. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you have any questions about this vacancy or the application process, please email Estates Division on EDR@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference XM50899 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information - Food Service Assistant Key information Department/location Estates Division (Business Services) Salary £25,249 Reference XM50899 Category Assistant staff Date published 8 September 2026 Closing date 6 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/fully-funded-phd-studentship-discovery-of-rational-therapeutic-biomarkers-in-breast-cancer-by-sw50984": {
      "title": "Fully-funded PhD studentship: Discovery of rational therapeutic biomarkers in breast cancer by systems pathology and deep learning",
      "description": "Fully-funded PhD studentship: Discovery of rational therapeutic biomarkers in breast cancer by systems pathology and deep learning | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Fully-funded PhD studentship: Discovery of rational therapeutic biomarkers in breast cancer by systems pathology and deep learning Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Fully-funded PhD studentship: Discovery of rational therapeutic biomarkers in breast cancer by systems pathology and deep learning Supervisor: Dr Hamid Raza Ali Department/location: Cancer Research UK Cambridge Institute Deadline for application: 16th October 2026 Course start date: 1st October 2027 Overview The Ali Lab wishes to recruit a student to work on the project entitled: \"Discovery of rational therapeutic biomarkers in breast cancer by systems pathology and deep learning\". For further information about the research group, including their most recent publications, please visit their website at www.ali-lab.co.uk/ Project details The therapeutic landscape for breast cancer patients is rapidly evolving with novel therapies regularly receiving regulatory approval. Yet directing these treatments to patients likely to benefit while sparing those unlikely to respond from their toxicities remains a major challenge. Many modern therapies, like immunotherapy and ADCs, rely on tissue architecture to be effective. Intercellular relationships in breast cancer tissues also determine cellular activation states and expression profiles, rendering some cells susceptible and others resistant to new treatments. The aim of this project is to use modern multiomic spatial methods (in which our group has extensive expertise1¿4) together with deep learning (for efficient representation and cross-modal learning) to discover the potential therapeutic landscape for novel therapies in breast cancer, and to propose rational multidimensional biomarkers for combinatorial therapy. We are generating multimodal spatial datasets in cohorts of breast cancer patients (the largest of their kind; making extensive use of imaging mass cytometry and spatial transcriptomics) that span observational studies and clinical trials. We must precisely define the landscape of novel target expression, quantify its heterogeneity, and the contribution of tissue architecture as a determinant of expression profiles. This project will involve large scale data processing and analysis in a setting with ample expertise and infrastructure. This is a rare opportunity to develop expertise in quantitative pathology in the burgeoning field of spatial cancer biology. Ours is a diverse and collaborative group that spans clinicians, pathologists, computational and cancer biologists. You will receive extensive training in cancer pathology, highly multiplexed imaging, and predictive modelling. References/further reading Wang, X. Q. et al. Spatial predictors of immunotherapy response in triple-negative breast cancer. Nature 621, 868-876 (2023). Danenberg, E. et al. Breast tumor microenvironment structures are associated with genomic features and clinical outcome. Nat Genet 54, 660-669 (2022). Ali, H. R. et al. Imaging mass cytometry and multiplatform genomics define the phenogenomic landscape of breast cancer. Nat Cancer 1, 163-175 (2020). Gupta, P. et al. Single-cell spatial atlas of the aging human breast. Nat Aging https://doi.org/10.1038/s43587-026-01104-3 (2026) doi:10.1038/s43587-026-01104-3. Preferred skills/knowledge Applications are invited from graduates in quantitative disciplines such as computer science, AI, and mathematics, but we also encourage applications from biologists and clinicians already experienced in computational methods. How to apply Please apply via the University Applicant Portal. For further information about the course and to access the Applicant Portal, visit: https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/courses/directory/cvcrpdmsc You should select to commence study in October 2027. References We would appreciate it if you could ask your referees to submit their references as soon as possible upon request, despite the longer University deadline for references. They will receive a request once you have completed the References section of your application. Please quote reference SW50984 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Cancer Research UK Cambridge Institute Salary Reference SW50984 Category Studentships Date published 9 September 2026 Closing date 16 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/fully-funded-phd-studentship-innate-adaptive-immune-interactions-in-cancer-evolution-sw51021": {
      "title": "Fully-funded PhD studentship: Innate-adaptive immune interactions in cancer evolution",
      "description": "Fully-funded PhD studentship: Innate-adaptive immune interactions in cancer evolution | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Fully-funded PhD studentship: Innate-adaptive immune interactions in cancer evolution Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Fully-funded PhD studentship: Innate-adaptive immune interactions in cancer evolution Supervisor: Dr Tim Halim Department/location: Cancer Research UK Cambridge Institute Deadline for application: 16th October 2026 Course start date: 1st October 2027 Project details For further information about the research group, please visit our website at https://www.cruk.cam.ac.uk/research-groups/halim-group/ The immune system is capable of detecting cancer, which forms the basis of immunotherapy. While immunotherapy has revolutionised cancer treatment in recent years, it remains unclear how exactly the immue system detects and responds to pre-cancereous lesions. Nevertheless, it is known that the activatin (or deactivation) of anti-cancer immune cells involves many different cell-types that communicate dynamically in specific tissue niches. Our laboratory has an interest in resolving the interactions between tissue-resident immune cells, with the overall goal of identifying key mechanisms that can be targeted therapeutically at different stages of cancer. We found that recently discovered, tissue-resident, group 2 innate lymphoid cells (ILC2) can play key roles in coordinating different types of immune and non-immune cells that play critical roles in tumourigenesis (i.e. Tregs, Dendritic Cells, cancer associated fibroblasts, etc.). The project will start by asking if ILC2, or other tissue-resident innate immune cells, play an important role during tumour evolution. The PhD project will build on ongoing work in different cancer types (pancreatic, breast, ovarian and lung), where we have already developed mouse models and human tissue processing pipelines. The candidate will learn and use cutting edge immunological methods (high parameter flow cytometry, multiplex imaging, single cell and spatial transcriptomics) in conjuction with intravital imaging and proximity labelling tools to define the dynamic interaction of immune cells in cancer. The candidate will need to master both complex 'wet lab' techniques, as well as learn how to perform robust in silico analysis of data (training will be provided by senior lab members, collaborators, or experts in the Institute core facilities). The candidate will generate hypotheses from these large descriptive datasets, which will then be tested rigorously using in vitro assays, in vivo, and ultimately in patient derived material. The overall ambition is to uncover novel mechanistic insights about immune regulation in early cancer, and to design interventions that halt or delay tumour progression. References/further reading Yip T et al. Science 2026 (DOI: 10.1126/science.aea5113) Stockis J et al. Science Immunology 2024 (DOI: 10.1126/sciimmunol.adl1903) Schuijs MJ et al. Nature Immunology 2020 ( https://doi.org/10.1038/s41590-020-0745-y ) Preferred skills/knowledge Cell biology / Immunology laboratory experience, such as: Flow cytometry and FACS, immunofluorescence microscopy/imaging, cell culture, tumour cell killing assays, tissue processing, etc. Molecular biology laboratory experience, such as: DNA/RNA extraction, PCR, cloning, virus production, ELISA, etc. Computational biology laboratory experience, such as: bulk- singl cell- or spatial-transcriptomic data analysis, spatial biology analysis using HALO, Imaris, etc. Murine or human tissue work, such as: tissue processing, tumour or immune-related in vivo models, surgical skills, transgenic animal models, UK PIL training, etc. Knowledge: background in cancer biology, immunology, cell biology, etc. Training and interest in bioinformatics, computational biology. How to apply Please apply via the University Applicant Portal at: https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/courses/directory/cvcrpdmsc You should select to commence study in October 2027. References We would appreciate it if you could ask your referees to submit their references as soon as possible upon request, despite the longer University deadline for references. They will receive a request once you have completed the References section of your application. Please quote reference SW51021 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Cancer Research UK Cambridge Institute Salary Reference SW51021 Category Studentships Date published 9 September 2026 Closing date 16 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom 