    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/fully-funded-phd-studentship-understanding-the-identity-and-function-of-tumour-initiating-states-sw51026": {
      "title": "Fully-funded PhD studentship: Understanding the identity and function of tumour-initiating states",
      "description": "Fully-funded PhD studentship: Understanding the identity and function of tumour-initiating states | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Fully-funded PhD studentship: Understanding the identity and function of tumour-initiating states Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Fully-funded PhD studentship: Understanding the identity and function of tumour-initiating states Supervisor: Prof Masashi Narita Department/location: Cancer Research UK Cambridge Institute Deadline for application: 16th October 2026 Course start date: 1st October 2027 Project details For further information about the research group, including their most recent publications, please visit their website at https://www.naritalab.com/ Our laboratory investigates the biology of preneoplastic cell states, with particular interest in how changes in cell functional identity and plasticity are controlled by the epigenome. We are interested in how oncogenic and metabolic signalling reshapes chromatin organisation and gene regulation, how these changes influence cellular fitness and tissue microenvironment, and which states ultimately suppress or promote tumour development. The PhD project will investigate the molecular and epigenetic mechanisms underlying transitions between preneoplastic cell states using diverse models, both in vitro and in vivo. The precise direction of the project will be developed according to the student's interests and emerging findings in the laboratory. Depending on the specific questions addressed, approaches may include mammalian cell culture, molecular and cell biology, genetic perturbation, imaging, epigenetic and chromatin profiling, and in vivo models of early tumorigenesis. Genomic and single-cell approaches may also be used, supported by collaboration with computational scientists. References/further reading Zhu H, Chan ASL & Narita M. The rise of RAS: how gradual oncogene activation shapes the OIS spectrum. Genes & Development (2025). Chan ASL et al. Oncogenic RAS drives a spectrum of cellular states with distinct tumour-propagating potential. Nature (2024). Olan I, Ando-Kuri M, Parry AJ et al. HMGA1 orchestrates chromatin compartmentalization and sequesters genes into 3D networks coordinating senescence heterogeneity. Nature Communications 15, 6891 (2024). Cassidy LD et al. Temporal inhibition of autophagy reveals segmental reversal of ageing with increased cancer risk. Nature Communications 11, 307 (2020). Olan I, Handa T & Narita M. Beyond SAHF: an integrative view of chromatin compartmentalization during senescence. Trends in Cell Biology (2023). Preferred skills/knowledge Previous hands-on laboratory research experience is highly desirable. Experience in one or more of the following would be advantageous but is not essential: mammalian cell culture, molecular biology, microscopy, genetic manipulation, chromatin or epigenetic assays, and cancer or ageing model systems. The successful candidate should have a strong interest in mechanistic biology and in using experimental approaches to understand fundamental questions in cancer development. Computational or bioinformatics expertise is advantageous but not required. Training and collaborative support will be available to analyse genomic datasets generated during the project. Eligibility We welcome applications from both UK and overseas students. Applications are invited from recent graduates or final-year undergraduates who hold or expect to gain a First/Upper Second Class degree (or equivalent) in a relevant subject from any recognised university worldwide. Applicants with relevant research experience, gained through Master's study or while working in a laboratory, are strongly encouraged to apply. How to apply Please apply via the University Applicant Portal. For further information about the course and to access the Applicant Portal, visit: https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/courses/directory/cvcrpdmsc You should select to commence study in October 2027. References We would appreciate it if you could ask your referees to submit their references as soon as possible upon request, despite the longer University deadline for references. They will receive a request once you have completed the References section of your application. Please quote reference SW51026 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Cancer Research UK Cambridge Institute Salary Reference SW51026 Category Studentships Date published 9 September 2026 Closing date 16 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/fully-funded-phd-studentship-unmasking-mechanisms-of-resistance-to-kras-inhibition-across-pancreatic-sw51012": {
      "title": "Fully-funded PhD studentship: Unmasking mechanisms of resistance to KRAS inhibition across pancreatic cancer genetic profiles",
      "description": "Fully-funded PhD studentship: Unmasking mechanisms of resistance to KRAS inhibition across pancreatic cancer genetic profiles | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Fully-funded PhD studentship: Unmasking mechanisms of resistance to KRAS inhibition across pancreatic cancer genetic profiles Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Fully-funded PhD studentship: Unmasking mechanisms of resistance to KRAS inhibition across pancreatic cancer genetic profiles Supervisor: Dr Giulia Biffi Department/location: Cancer Research UK Cambridge Institute Deadline for application: 16th October 2026 Course start date: 1st October 2027 Project details For further information about the research group, including their most recent publications, please visit their website at https://biffilab.wordpress.com Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) remains a formidable clinical challenge. A major recent advance has been the development of KRAS inhibitors, which have shown considerable promise in clinical trials (1). For decades, KRAS was considered undruggable; however, these new inhibitors represent a potential turning point for patients with PDAC. Despite this progress, rapid therapy resistance has already been observed in both patients and pre-clinical mouse models, highlighting the urgent need to define the mechanisms that drive resistance and to identify rational combination therapies. In our laboratory, we have established and validated a panel of genetically heterogeneous PDAC models. These studies have shown that malignant-cell genetics shape distinct, therapeutically actionable vulnerabilities through both cell-intrinsic programmes and changes in the surrounding tumour microenvironment [(2) and unpublished]. These observations create an important opportunity: we hypothesise that malignant¿stromal crosstalk within defined genetic contexts drives distinct mechanisms of resistance to KRAS inhibition. In support of this, our work indicates that cancer-associated fibroblasts play diverse roles in PDAC progression and activate pathways that may contribute to therapy resistance (3, 4). This PhD project will therefore identify malignant cell-intrinsic and malignant cell-extrinsic mechanisms of resistance to KRAS inhibition, including fibroblast-mediated mechanisms, across genetically distinct PDACs. We will prioritise PDAC genotypes for which we already have preliminary data suggesting candidate mechanisms of resistance. The project will combine complementary models and techniques, including co-transplantation of organoids in mouse models, in vivo CRISPR, single-cell RNA sequencing, flow cytometry, multiplex immunofluorescence, spatial transcriptomics, and standard molecular biology approaches. A computational component may also be available, depending on the skills and interests of the student. Together, these strategies will define how intra-tumour genetic heterogeneity influences response and resistance to KRAS inhibition, complementing ongoing studies in the laboratory investigating inter-tumour genetic heterogeneity. References/further reading Daraxonrasib or Chemotherapy in Previously Treated Metastatic Pancreatic Cancer. N Engl J Med. 2026. (PMID: 42223072) SMAD4 and KRAS status shape malignant-stromal crosstalk in pancreatic cancer. Cancer Res. 2025. (PMID: 39841099) EGFR-activated myofibroblasts promote metastasis of pancreatic cancer. Cancer Cell. 2024. (PMID: 38157863) Functional heterogeneity of fibroblasts in primary tumours and metastases. Trends in Cancer. 2024. (PMID: 39674792) Preferred skills/knowledge We are looking for a motivated student who is excited by this project, enthusiastic about joining a collaborative and hard-working laboratory, and committed to the complementary experimental strategies required for its successful completion. As this project will rely heavily on mouse models, willingness to work with mouse models throughout the PhD is essential. Prior experience in a laboratory or industry research environment is required. Experience with tissue culture, including cell lines and/or organoids, common laboratory techniques such as immunohistochemistry, western blotting and/or PCR, and work with mouse models would be viewed favourably. Prior experience or knowledge of pancreatic cancer or cancer biology would also be advantageous. Eligibility We welcome applications from both UK and overseas students. How to apply Please apply via the University Applicant Portal at https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/courses/directory/cvcrpdmsc You should select to commence study in October 2027. Please quote reference SW51012 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Cancer Research UK Cambridge Institute Salary Reference SW51012 Category Studentships Date published 9 September 2026 Closing date 16 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/health-safety-and-wellbeing-manager-ba50988": {
      "title": "Health, Safety and Wellbeing Manager",
      "description": "Health, Safety and Wellbeing Manager | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Health, Safety and Wellbeing Manager Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Health, Safety and Wellbeing Manager The Role: Health, Safety and Wellbeing Manager This role holder is key to ensuring statutory compliance and driving a positive safety culture throughout the Estates Division and the wider University. Specifically, the post holder will be accountable for drafting, developing, maintaining and communicating health and safety codes of practice, guidance and standards. They are also responsible for establishing processes and for monitoring and reporting on health, safety and wellbeing performance across all services and functions provided and managed by the Estates Division.  These include construction project works, building maintenance activities, accommodation services, housing operations, land management, catering, waste management and the dairy farm.  The remit involves direct interfaces with Department leads, employed staff, external contractors, and service providers. The role acts as an expert health safety and wellbeing advisor, setting standards for monitoring health and safety performance including that of consultants and contractors. The role also involves leading and supporting safety and wellbeing projects, developing/managing systems, analysing performance, designing and delivering training/briefings, engaging and motivating University and Estates Division staff to work without breach of statutory compliance and with attention to reduced risk. About you: A successful applicant will have: sound working knowledge of health and safety legislation and best practice the ability to identify risks and advise on risk solutions in compliance with legislation and standards experience of developing policy and procedures in compliance with relevant legislation and standards proven experience of undertaking and reviewing risk assessments in compliance with national and corporate standards experience of project leadership, including developing and implementing staff development and training programmes ability to interpret the requirements of legislation, guidance and standards and apply these to a large, complex, multi-site organisation. excellent communication skills, be a team player, with a strong customer focus ability to think flexibly, simulate and analyse information quickly and make reasoned judgements using pragmatic and effective decision making and problem solving. They will hold a NEBOSH national diploma (or equivalent level 6 qualification) and be at least a graduate level member of the Institution of Occupational Safety and Health (or other professional and informative organisation). In return we offer an encouraging and supportive environment, a salary of between £47,389 and £59,966 (depending on experience), an attractive pension scheme, generous holiday allowance and many other employee benefits. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you have any questions about this vacancy or the application process, please email Estates Division on EDR@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference BA50988 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information - Health Safety and Wellbeing Manager Key information Department/location Estates Division Salary £47,389-£59,966 Reference BA50988 Category Academic-related Date published 8 September 2026 Closing date 22 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/hpc-support-analyst-vc51034": {
      "title": "HPC Support Analyst",
      "description": "HPC Support Analyst | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs HPC Support Analyst Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs HPC Support Analyst Are you interested in an exciting and unique opportunity to support the UK's HPC and AI Research Services?  We are looking for enthusiastic individuals to join our team operating large HPC and AI infrastructure including one of the UK's fastest AI supercomputers, Zenith. We support scientists across the UK in critical research fields such as clean energy, personalised medicine and climate science and you will help us in delivering world class services. Who you'll be working with The role is based in Research Computing Services, a leading UK National Supercomputing Centre, providing facilities and services to world-renowned scientists, clinicians and engineers across the UK and Europe. You'll be part of a growing team of specialist, Software and Systems Engineers, working with a wide range of stakeholders across multiple disciplines and institutions. What you will do You'll be responsible for supporting a diverse range of activities, including providing first line support for all specialised Research computing IT services, user administration, problem solving, issue diagnosis and fault resolution, improving documentation, automating processes, contributing to training materials and events and business processes such as the processing of purchase orders and generation of reports, to ensure effective exploitation of the services by the researchers. Skills and experience you will need to demonstrate Proven experience in technical problem solving. Knowledge in one of the following areas: \nLinux system administration, scripting, atomation and configuration management (Ansible, Puppet), version control, cloud technologies e.g. OpenStack or VMware. Able to communicate effectively orally and in writing. Highly proactive and enthusiastic. An interest in working with AI, High Performance Computing or Research Software is essential but experience in these fields is not crucial as long as you show willingness to learn. What we can offer 36 days holiday per year including bank holidays. A generous pension. Hybrid working - you can work some of the time from home, coming into the office as and when required. A friendly and supportive team environment. The opportunity to shape and influence how we work. More information about the role is attached in the 'Further Particulars' document. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure (criminal records check) check and a security check. We support flexible and family-friendly working and are open to non-standard working patterns and to applicants who live outside Cambridge but are willing to travel to Cambridge when required. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you would like more information about this role, informal enquiries can be directed by email to recruitment@uis.cam.ac.uk . Please quote reference VC51034 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location University Information Services Salary £33,951-£39,906 Reference VC51034 Category Assistant staff Date published 9 September 2026 Closing date 9 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/hr-administrator-casual-workforce-part-time-ah50688": {
      "title": "HR Administrator (Casual Workforce) (Part Time)",
      "description": "HR Administrator (Casual Workforce) (Part Time) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs HR Administrator (Casual Workforce) (Part Time) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs HR Administrator (Casual Workforce) (Part Time) The University of Cambridge is globally recognised as a leading centre for research, education, and scholarship. Employing more than 13,000 staff across academic, research, and professional services roles, the University relies on the expertise and dedication of its people to maintain and strengthen its world-leading reputation. Recognising that its staff are its greatest asset, the University is committed to creating an environment where everyone can thrive, ensuring the continued success of its teaching, research, and wider mission. The Casual Worker HR Team is part of the HR Shared Service and provides a comprehensive support service to University departments in managing the University's casual workforce. The team offers expert advice and guidance on the use of the Cambridge Casual Worker System (CCWS), which is used to manage key aspects of casual worker administration, including right-to-work compliance, booking casual work, processing timesheets, and coordinating payroll. We are a small, friendly, and supportive team committed to delivering a high standard of customer service to our colleagues. We work collaboratively with departments to support a more strategic approach to planning and managing the University's casual workforce, while promoting consistency and best practice across the organisation. The Role: As an HR Administrator within the Casual Worker HR Team, you will support the Casual Worker HR Manager by providing a wide range of administrative support related to the University's casual workforce. Your responsibilities will include onboarding new casual workers, maintaining data integrity across HR systems, reviewing reports, processing leavers, and responding to a variety of enquiries. The role also offers the opportunity to contribute to key projects, including the annual Right to Work audit and the implementation of changes arising from employment legislation and University policies and guidance. This is a busy and varied role, supporting approximately 700 departmental users and 12,000 casual workers across 130 departments. The scope of the role is expected to expand further as the team takes on additional responsibilities relating to examination activity across the University. About you: Applicants should be educated to A-level standard (or equivalent) or possess a comparable level of practical experience. You should have experience working in an administrative environment and be proficient in using Microsoft Office applications, including Outlook, Word, and Excel. Experience of using query management systems and/or HR systems would be advantageous. To succeed in this role, you will need to thrive in a fast-paced environment, remaining calm under pressure while managing multiple priorities and working to tight deadlines. Strong attention to detail is essential, together with excellent organisational skills and a commitment to delivering a consistently high standard of customer service. Further information about the role and its responsibilities is available via the link below. Benefits of working at Cambridge include: Competitive rates of pay with automatic service-related pay progression and annual cost of living increases. Generous annual leave allowance of 36 days paid leave (including bank holidays) The University of Cambridge is committed to supporting our staff to thrive both professionally and personally. We aim to support a good work/life balance, whilst retaining the positive aspects of our unique in-person environment and culture.\nElements of this role are suitable for hybrid working, where staff can split their time between in-person collaboration and remote working. The frequency of the remote elements of the role can be discussed with the recruiting manager. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure (criminal records check) check and a security check. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Closing date for applications: Midnight, Wednesday 30th September 2026 Interviews for the role will be held: Friday 16th October 2026 Please quote reference AH50688 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information Key information Department/location Human Resources Division Salary £27,319-£31,236 pro rata Reference AH50688 Category Assistant staff Date published 11 September 2026 Closing date 30 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/hr-advisor-fixed-term-ra50957": {
      "title": "HR Advisor (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "HR Advisor (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs HR Advisor (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs HR Advisor (Fixed Term) Contract: Fixed-term until 31 March 2029 Salary: £33,951 - £39,906 per annum Location: Cambridge Biomedical Campus, with hybrid working Working Pattern: Full Time Are you an HR professional who enjoys supporting organisational change and helping teams navigate complex people challenges? We're looking for an HR Adviser to join our Human Resources Division and play a key role in supporting Medical Research Council (MRC) units across the Schools of Clinical Medicine and Biological Sciences during a significant period of organisational change and transformation. We encourage early applications as they will be reviewed on a rolling basis. The vacancy may close before the advertised deadline if a sufficient number of suitable applications are received. About the role Working closely with the HR Business Partner, you'll provide expert HR support across a diverse portfolio of research units, helping leaders and managers deliver change while maintaining a positive employee experience. This is a varied role combining employee relations, workforce planning, organisational change, recruitment, employee engagement and HR project work. You'll build strong relationships with stakeholders across the University, NHS and MRC, helping to deliver practical HR solutions that support world-leading research. What you'll be doing Supporting organisational change and workforce planning initiatives across MRC-funded departments. Advising managers on HR policies, employee relations matters and people-related challenges. Assisting with investigations, casework and change projects. Supporting staff engagement, learning and development initiatives. Contributing to equality, diversity and inclusion activities. Building effective relationships with internal and external stakeholders, including NHS and MRC partners. We're looking for someone who has Experience working in a generalist or specialist HR environment. Good knowledge of HR best practice and employment legislation. Experience supporting employee relations matters and responding to HR queries. Strong communication and influencing skills. The ability to manage competing priorities and build effective stakeholder relationships. Experience of organisational change, project work, or workforce initiatives would be particularly valuable. Why join us? Alongside working in a collaborative and supportive environment, the University of Cambridge offers: Excellent development opportunities and career progression. Generous pension contributions and 36 days annual leave. Hybrid and flexible working arrangements. Discounts at local businesses and major retailers. A range of health and wellbeing benefits and family-friendly policies. Fixed-term: Funding is available until 31 March 2029 in the first instance. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure (criminal records check) check, a health assessment, a security check and an enhanced Disclosure and Barring Service check. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. Closing date: Monday 5th October 2026 For this role, all applications will be managed by Spirehouse Recruitment. To apply, please send your CV to cambridge@spirehouse.co.uk . For further information about the role, please contact Abbie Carr (Executive Consultant) at abbie@spirehouse.co.uk . The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Download further information Further Information Key information Department/location Office of the School of Clinical Medicine Salary £33,951-£39,906 Reference RA50957 Category Assistant staff Date published 4 September 2026 Closing date 5 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/it-principal-engineer-servers-and-storage-vc51005": {
      "title": "IT Principal Engineer (Servers and Storage)",
      "description": "IT Principal Engineer (Servers and Storage) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs IT Principal Engineer (Servers and Storage) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs IT Principal Engineer (Servers and Storage) The role In this role you will administer Linux servers, and storage systems, in private and public clouds, along with security compliance and monitoring work. You will also provide advice and guidance on security strategies, managing identified risks and enabling the adoption of standards. The Servers & Storage team offers a supportive and collaborative environment, and you will get to work alongside subject-matter experts. You will have the opportunity to learn new skills and develop your career. What you will do As an IT Principal Engineer responsible for Linux servers, you will: Work within a team to support and enhance and transform our core IT server infrastructure - both physical and virtual. Contribute to the development of information security policy, standards and guidelines. Implement and support key technologies in server, virtualisation, storage, and software - utilising automation and scripting where possible. Ensure that business continuity measures are implemented and maintained to minimize the impact of systems failure. Monitor, diagnose and proactively fix issues. Own in-team technical projects associated with the improvement of infrastructure. Implement and maintain automated systems to administer systems and services at scale. Ensure that systems and procedural documentation is maintained and accurate. Work closely with people across UIS and wider University, including software engineers, networks engineers, security staff, database administrators, project managers and service teams. Design systems / choose appropriate technologies to solve the needs of one of the leading academic institutions in the world. Ensure your technical skills are kept up to date. What we are looking for The successful applicant will have demonstrable experience working with the following products and technologies: Linux (RHEL or Ubuntu) systems administration. Experience of running and monitoring secure systems. Strong scripting and automation skills - Python experience would be particularly valuable. Configuration / code management (eg Ansible).VMware hypervisor, networking and automation. NAS and SAN storage systems. Full stack understanding of how different layers of IT interoperate, from networking up to the application / service level. Strong troubleshooting skills. Good communication, organisational and problem-solving skills. The following skills/knowledge would also be useful: IT Compliance Monitoring and Management (e.g. Tanium). Endpoint Detection and Response (e.g. SentinelOne). Email routing. Web technologies (Apache, Tomcat, traffic management, search engines). Databases (esp. PostgreSQL, MySQL). VMware Cloud Foundation or its component technologies. NAS and SAN storage systems. Comfortable finding solutions across technology boundaries. Degree (2:1 or higher) or equivalent work experience. The team is flexible and supportive, and we endeavour to allow employees to focus on the technologies they wish to where possible. We support flexible and family-friendly working and are open to non-standard working patterns. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you would like more information about this role enquiries can be directed by email to recruitment@uis.cam.ac.uk . Please quote reference VC51005 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location University Information Services Salary £47,389-£59,966 Reference VC51005 Category Academic-related Date published 10 September 2026 Closing date 28 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/it-technician-rw51039": {
      "title": "IT Technician",
      "description": "IT Technician | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs IT Technician Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs IT Technician Do you have great IT and organisational skills?  Interested in a role which is contributing to changing lives for the better? Looking for your next challenge? We are recruiting an IT Technician with experience of working in an IT department. You will need recent experience in delivering a service and evidence of working at a tactical or operational level to deliver a technical solution as well as having the ability to manage and prioritise own tasks to succeed in this role. About NIHR BioResource: Our mission is to facilitate human health research and its transformation into medical practice. NIHR BioResource is a unique and powerful resource for studying disease mechanisms and for investigating the links between genes, the environment, health and disease, enabling scientific discoveries as well as facilitating translational medicine for the benefit of patients. Further details on the NIHR BioResource, including some of our COVID-19 research studies, can be found on our website https://bioresource.nihr.ac.uk . About the job: NIHR Bioresource IT is a dynamic department implementing significant change across the organisation. This change and increased level of expectations have led to a need to provide more skilled responses to support NIHR Bioresource staff and systems. This role will assist in the delivery of a professional IT service enabling the ongoing delivery of best practice processes going forward. The post joins an IT service team providing IT support to the BioResource. The post holder will be involved in all aspects of IT. The role will suit a responsible person with experience of working in IT support, looking to progress their skills. The NIHR BioResource is committed to providing training to allow the right candidate to progress. The role holder takes responsibility for providing first- and second-line support for the IT services of the department, including the problem solving of routine technical issues. The role holder will also support key data flows and processes and assist in the configuration and development of key tools in this process. About you: Ideally you will have: \n- Demonstrable experience in a specific area of IT\n Developing knowledge of one technical specialism within one IT domain e.g. Desktop Support within the IT domain of infrastructure \n- Ability to communicate effectively orally and in writing What we offer: The University offers a wide range of benefits including flexible working, family friendly policies, an attractive pension scheme (employer's contribution is 12.6%) and 36 days' annual holiday (inclusive of public holidays). We encourage career development by providing extensive training & development opportunities. This appointment is full-time, 36.5 hours per week; part-time to 80% will be considered. This role will be office-based 4 days a week, with the option to work remotely 1 day a week. The post is available on a fixed-term basis until 31 March 2028 in the first instance. NIHR BioResource is funded by the National Institute for Health & Care Research (NIHR) on a 4-year funding cycle. Our offices are on the Cambridge Biomedical Campus CB2 0QQ (approximately 2 miles south of the city centre). See the Further Information link below for full details and Person Specification. This appointment requires a Research Passport application. We encourage informal queries about the job or the application process - please contact Silvia Lo, IT Service Manager, Silvia.Lo@bioresource.nihr.ac.uk . Closing date: 3 weeks from Advert Interview date: w/c 5th October 2026 Limited funding: The funds for this post are available until 31 March 2028 in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role on the online application form. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference RW51039 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars (RW51039) Key information Department/location Department of Haematology, NIHR BioResource Research Group Salary £31,236-£35,608 Reference RW51039 Category Assistant staff Date published 10 September 2026 Closing date 28 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/knowledge-exchange-coordinator-fixed-term-ps50969": {
      "title": "Knowledge Exchange Coordinator (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Knowledge Exchange Coordinator (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Knowledge Exchange Coordinator (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Knowledge Exchange Coordinator (Fixed Term) The Knowledge Exchange Coordinator will be based in the Cambridge Stem Cell Institute (CSCI) and will support the Senior Knowledge Exchange Manager to deliver knowledge exchange activities for the recently awarded UKRI National Hub (£25M) held in the CSCI.\nThe UK Hub for Predictive and AI-Enabled Human Disease Models is a UK national research initiative funded by MRC, which will translate academic New Approach Methodologies (NAMs) into validated, industry-ready systems. Translation and Knowledge Transfer and Exchange (KTE) are core operational functions across the Hub, forming the link between academia, SMEs, industry, NHS partners, regulators, charities, and patient communities, with translation embedded throughout all operational stages. The purpose of the Knowledge Exchange Coordinator is to provide high-quality administrative support for KTE activities across the Hub and CSCI. They will work closely with the Senior Knowledge Exchange Manager and other institute professional services staff to ensure a high-quality, proactive, and effective KTE support service.\nWe are seeking a proactive and highly organised Knowledge Exchange Coordinator to play a central support role in the delivery and development of our ambitious KTE programme. Key Responsibilities will include support and planning of KTE activities, operational input, administration and delivery of KTE objectives.\nThe successful candidate will have previous knowledge exchange or related relevant administrative support experience and excellent customer service skills. An understanding of the biomedical research sector either in Higher Education or commercial organisations would also be helpful. They will be comfortable operating in a fast-paced academic environment and able to engage effectively with a wide range of stakeholders, including researchers, clinicians, funders, and external commercial partners. Full-time (1.0 FTE; change to part time and flexible working arrangements available); Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available until 31 March 2030 in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role on the online application form.\nPlease include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference PS50969 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Cambridge Stem Cell Institute (SBS) Salary £31,236-£35,608 Reference PS50969 Category Assistant staff Date published 4 September 2026 Closing date 1 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/laboratory-purchasing-and-facilities-technician-rc51025": {
      "title": "Laboratory, Purchasing and Facilities Technician",
      "description": "Laboratory, Purchasing and Facilities Technician | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Laboratory, Purchasing and Facilities Technician Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Laboratory, Purchasing and Facilities Technician We have an exciting opportunity to join this busy department in a key role as a Laboratory Purchasing and Facilities Technician which underpins activities within our research laboratories based in the hospital. The successful candidate will support the Laboratory & Facilities Coordinator in the oversight and management of procurement of goods and other services on behalf of the Department and/or research groups in order to ensure smooth running of the Goods-in Store's service. You will monitor stocks of compressed gases, liquid nitrogen, dry ice, including with assisting a liquid nitrogen Cell Bank, oversee glass washing and waste stream disposal (clinical, WEEE, chemical etc). In house training will be provided to have significant hands-on experience in facilities support and to gain working experience within a laboratory and hospital setting. This is a very varied role where no two days are the same, so we are looking for someone who can be adaptable and remain calm in situations where priorities can change at short notice. The role involves some manual handling and so a good level of physical fitness is essential. For informal enquiries please contact Anders Jensen alj54@medschl.cam.ac.uk Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role on the online application form. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference RC51025 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particular Key information Department/location Department of Medicine Salary £25,249-£27,319 Reference RC51025 Category Assistant staff Date published 9 September 2026 Closing date 6 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/lead-hr-business-partner-internal-only-ah50954": {
      "title": "Lead HR Business Partner (Internal Only)",
      "description": "Lead HR Business Partner (Internal Only) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Lead HR Business Partner (Internal Only) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Lead HR Business Partner (Internal Only) Contract: Permanent Salary: £63,606 - £67,468 per annum Location: Cambridge, with hybrid working Working Pattern: Full Time Please note this role is only available to internal applicants. Are you a strategic HR leader who thrives on influencing senior stakeholders, leading organisational change, and delivering people solutions that make a lasting impact? We're looking for a Lead HR Business Partner to join the Human Resources Division and provide strategic HR leadership within either the School of Arts & Humanities or the School of Humanities and Social Sciences. This is an opportunity to work with senior leaders across a world-leading institution, helping shape people strategies that support academic excellence and organisational success. About the role As a trusted adviser to senior management teams, you'll lead the development and delivery of strategic HR initiatives, ensuring people priorities are aligned with institutional goals. You'll play a key role in leading organisational change, managing complex employee relations matters, delivering major HR projects, and influencing decision-making at the highest levels. This is a highly visible leadership position where you'll combine strategic thinking with practical delivery, working collaboratively with stakeholders across the University to drive positive change and organisational effectiveness. What you'll be doing Leading the HR contribution to strategic and operational planning. Partnering with senior leaders to identify and deliver people priorities. Leading organisational change programmes and workforce initiatives. Advising on complex and high-risk employee relations matters. Delivering University-wide and local HR projects. Building strong relationships with key stakeholders and trade union colleagues. Using workforce data and insight to support informed decision-making. Providing leadership and direction to HR teams supporting your client areas. We're looking for someone who has Significant HR experience at a senior level within a complex organisation. A strong track record of leading organisational change and influencing senior stakeholders. Experience developing and implementing strategic people solutions. Excellent communication, negotiation and relationship-building skills. Experience managing complex employee relations issues. Strong analytical skills and the ability to translate business priorities into HR actions. A collaborative leadership style and a commitment to continuous improvement. CIPD membership or equivalent professional experience would be advantageous. Why join us? Alongside working in a collaborative and intellectually stimulating environment, the University of Cambridge offers: Excellent opportunities for professional development and career progression. Generous pension provision and 41 days annual leave including public holidays. Hybrid and flexible working arrangements. Access to CAMbens employee benefits and retail discounts. A wide range of health and wellbeing support services. The chance to play a key role in shaping the future of one of the world's leading universities. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure, security check and health assessment. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Closing date: Friday 18 September 2026 This vacancy is open to internal applicants only. Informal enquiries and queries regarding the application process are welcomed and should be directed to the resourcingqueries@admin.cam.ac.uk . Please quote reference AH50954 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information Key information Department/location Human Resources Division Salary £63,606-£67,468 Reference AH50954 Category Academic-related Date published 4 September 2026 Closing date 18 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/leverhulme-centre-for-life-in-the-universe-lclu-phd-studentship-fixed-term-ka51068": {
      "title": "Leverhulme Centre for Life in the Universe (LCLU) PhD Studentship (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Leverhulme Centre for Life in the Universe (LCLU) PhD Studentship (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Leverhulme Centre for Life in the Universe (LCLU) PhD Studentship (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Leverhulme Centre for Life in the Universe (LCLU) PhD Studentship (Fixed Term) Applications are invited for a fully funded, four-year PhD studentship, available for home (UK) students starting in October 2027.  The successful candidate will be based within one of the Centre's participating departments and will undertake a research project aligned with the Centre's mission to develop a deeper understanding of life, its origins, and its distribution throughout the Universe. Projects will contribute to one or more of the Centre's core research questions: What chemical pathways could have led to the origins of life under conditions compatible with diverse planetary environments? How can we identify and characterise environments on Earth and beyond that may serve as cradles for prebiotic chemistry and life? What observational facilities, technologies, and analytical methods will enable the study of planets beyond our Solar System and the detection of signatures of geological and biological evolution? How can philosophical and mathematical approaches refine our understanding of life and inspire new interdisciplinary modes of scientific enquiry? We are dedicated to promoting diversity and inclusivity and widening participation in postgraduate study.  Therefore, this studentship is only available to people who fulfil at least one of the eligibility criteria. For further information regarding eligibility see PhD Studentships (LCLU PhD Studentships ' Leverhulme Centre for Life in the Universe). This studentship will cover fees and stipend for home students at the UKRI rate, plus an LCLU uplift of £1,000 per year and research cost support will be provided. There is a list of available projects, but note that the list of projects is not exhaustive, and applicants are encouraged to approach any of our supervisors with their own project ideas.  If you would like to propose your own research topic, you can also browse through the list of supervisors with research interests to find those that particularly interest you. You should contact prospective supervisors before applying, to further explore your mutual interests, and to get additional advice and information. Participating Departments at the University of Cambridge: \nCavendish Laboratory, Institute of Astronomy, Department of Applied Mathematics and Theoretical Physics, Yusuf Hamied Department of Chemistry, Department of Biochemistry, Department of Earth Sciences, Department of Zoology, Department of History and Philosophy of Science, Faculty of Divinity, and the MRC Laboratory of Molecular Biology. Application process\nTo apply for a LCLU PhD Studentship, please apply through the University of Cambridge postgraduate admissions portal: https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/ and note in the applying for funding section that you are eligible for LCLU funding.  The materials required for your application will depend on the department of the primary PhD supervisor with details available here: https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/applying/how-do-i-apply . The deadlines for applications vary with the department, so please check what the relevant deadline is with the relevant PhD Supervisor, since some departments have earlier deadlines, such as 2nd December 2026.  All applications will have to be in by 12:00 pm (noon) GMT 8th December 2026. Application closing date: 8 December 2026. Interviews will be held within the week of 15-19 February 2027. For further enquiries please email: se413@cam.ac.uk Please quote reference KA51068 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Key information Department/location Department of Physics Salary Reference KA51068 Category Studentships Date published 14 September 2026 Closing date 8 December 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/marie-curie-ai-for-proteomics-phd-position-lilley-group-fixed-term-ph51013": {
      "title": "Marie Curie AI for proteomics PhD position (Lilley Group)",
      "description": "Marie Curie AI for proteomics PhD position (Lilley Group) - (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Marie Curie AI for proteomics PhD position (Lilley Group) - (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Marie Curie AI for proteomics PhD position (Lilley Group) - (Fixed Term) We are seeking applications for a PhD position in AI in proteomics as part of the Horizon Europe MSCA Doctoral Network, ProtAIomics. This brings together laboratories across Europe with 16 doctoral students at the interface of artificial intelligence and mass spectrometry proteomics. In collaboration with researchers at the University of Oxford, we seek a candidate to develop AI tools for the interrogation of subcellular proteomics data. We are interested to hear from candidates with experience in probabilistic or statistical modelling of complex biological data, such as mass spectrometry data, RNA sequencing, or imaging. The candidate should also have a background in working with hierarchical data and resolving ambiguous, overlapping signals. Strong programming skills are essential, along with the ability to build and extend statistical pipelines. An interest in Bayesian inference applied to biology is also important. A background in computational proteomics or LC-MS/MS analysis, especially peptide/protein identification or signal deconvolution, is desirable. Role Eligibility Requirements: Applicants must not have resided or carried out their main activity (work, studies, etc.) in the UK for more than 12 months in the 36 months immediately before their date of recruitment. Applicants must hold an academic degree that enables them to undertake doctoral studies. Applicants must not already be in possession of a doctoral degree. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available until from 9th November 2026, until 30th September 2029, in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. For informal enquiries, please contact Professor Kathryn Lilley ( https://www.bioc.cam.ac.uk/research/lilley ) via k.s.lilley@bioc.cam.ac.uk For queries regarding the application process, please contact the Biochemistry HR Team via: personnel@bioc.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference PH51013 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars - Marie Curie PhD Proteomics position (Lilley) Key information Department/location Department of Biochemistry Salary Reference PH51013 Category Research Date published 9 September 2026 Closing date 24 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/operations-lead-maternity-cover-part-time-nm51055": {
      "title": "Operations Lead (Maternity Cover) (Part Time)",
      "description": "Operations Lead (Maternity Cover) (Part Time) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Operations Lead (Maternity Cover) (Part Time) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Operations Lead (Maternity Cover) (Part Time) The Aviation Impact Accelerator (AIA) is seeking an experienced Operations Lead to provide maternity cover and ensure continuity of its day-to-day operations. Based at the Whittle Laboratory in West Cambridge and working with the AIA Director and wider team, you will coordinate operational priorities across areas including finance, HR, recruitment, resource planning and administration. You will also supervise operational support, resolve non-routine issues and provide clear information to support decision-making. We are looking for someone with strong organisational and coordination skills, good judgement, financial awareness and experience of working across multiple operational functions. Experience within a university, research or similarly complex environment would be an advantage. For full details of the role and person specification, please see the further particulars document. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 12 months in the first instance. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. The closing date for applications is Sunday 27 September 2026. Informal enquiries are welcomed and if you have any queries regarding either the vacancy or the application process, please contact the HR Office in the first instance on hr-office@eng.cam.ac.uk or 01223 332615. Please quote reference NM51055 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Engineering Salary £35,608-£46,049 pro rata Reference NM51055 Category Academic-related Date published 11 September 2026 Closing date 27 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/personal-assistant-secretary-internal-applicants-onlysecondment-ma50964": {
      "title": "Personal Assistant / Secretary (Internal Applicants only)(Secondment)",
      "description": "Personal Assistant / Secretary (Internal Applicants only)(Secondment) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Personal Assistant / Secretary (Internal Applicants only)(Secondment) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Personal Assistant / Secretary (Internal Applicants only)(Secondment) Fixed-term: 31 August 2027 We are looking for a well-organised person to provide comprehensive administrative support to small panel of 5 Professors and to their respective research groups when required.  In addition, the role holder will be the principal manager of the Department's Mifare Card Entry System. The successful applicant will have excellent communication and interpersonal skills and will demonstrate a high level of accuracy and attention to detail. The role holder will be able to prioritise and manage their own workload, sometimes against competing deadlines, and possess a high level of patience, tact, discretion, and confidentiality. They will be required to work both independently, acting on their own initiative, as well as part of a small support team.  An aptitude for team working and excellent communication is crucial. Duties will include managing the card access system: diary management; coordination of schedules; organising visits, seminars and lectures; arranging meetings both internally and externally with international participants; minute taking; liaising with members of the Department, University and external organisations. In addition, arranging travel and accommodation, recruitment and possibly financial management will be required. Ideally educated to A level standard or equivalent, the successful candidate will possess excellent IT skills including extensive knowledge of iOS software, website maintenance and Microsoft Office applications (Outlook, Word, Excel and PowerPoint).  A familiarity with Microsoft Teams is also essential, as is experience of managing a Mifare Card Entry System. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. For informal queries please contact Emma Graham ( ejg49@cam.ac.uk ). Please quote reference MA50964 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The Department holds an Athena SWAN silver award for women in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics, and Medicine. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further information Key information Department/location Yusuf Hamied Department of Chemistry Salary £27,319-£31,236 Reference MA50964 Category Assistant staff Date published 10 September 2026 Closing date 17 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/phd-studentship-in-human-computer-interaction-and-critical-computing-fixed-term-nr50923": {
      "title": "PhD Studentship in Human-Computer Interaction and Critical Computing (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "PhD Studentship in Human-Computer Interaction and Critical Computing (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs PhD Studentship in Human-Computer Interaction and Critical Computing (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs PhD Studentship in Human-Computer Interaction and Critical Computing (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 3.5 years. A doctoral studentship is available in the Aspirational Computing Lab in the Department of Computer Science and Technology (CST) at the University of Cambridge. The goal of this PhD programme is to launch one \"deceptive by design\" project that combines the perspectives of human-computer interaction (HCI) and critical computing. Topics of interest include: Persuasion and deception in conversational agents across and beyond text and voice modalities Drivers of influence and over trust in conversational agents that are charming and disarming Interventions against specific cases of interface deception, digital addiction, and/or overreliance on technology across digital platforms and products Other critical computing topics--including all matters of equality, diversity, and inclusion (ED&I or DEI), accessibility and inclusive design, feminism and intersectionality, trans and gender diversity studies, and misinformation and information deception--will also be considered. However, the topics must fall under the purview of the Aspirational Computing Lab and be supervisable by Dr. Seaborn. The applicant must propose a specific research question with an accompanying methodology and research plan that demonstrates their interest, knowledge, and ability to think as a researcher within the joint context of HCI and critical computing. In the first half year, the candidate will work closely with Dr. Seaborn and senior researchers to train and gain experience on the research topic and relevant methods. By the second half, the candidate will take on a leading role and begin carrying out the research comprising their doctoral dissertation. The candidate is expected to join other projects, participate in research communication and outreach, and take on other supporting roles. Notably, the candidate can be trained in small group teaching and invited to take on undergraduate supervisions.  Candidates must be self-motivated, collaboration-oriented, and open to interdisciplinary work and team contexts. Qualifications/Skills Holding a first-class honours degree or equivalent; a Master's-level degree (or other postgraduate work) is highly desirable; these should be relevant to HCI and/or critical computing and the proposed topic Solid understanding of HCI fundamentals and user-centred design Solid communication and interpersonal skills, including the ability to work with people of diverse backgrounds and across disciplinary conventions Solid understanding of research methods involving human participants, especially experimental design Basic understanding of data analysis approaches and tools, including statistics platforms like R and/or methods like thematic analysis Familiarity with deception within the context of computing and HCI Relevant publications, industrial output, or portfolio items, including open-source projects Practical skills in at least two of (a) programming and/or machine learning, (b) interaction design, and/or (c) research design Applicants must contact Dr. Seaborn ( katie.seaborn@cst.cam.ac.uk ) in advance to assess topic and background fit. In doing so, applicants must show evidence of engagement with this advert, Dr. Seaborn's research, and familiarization with the lab website (that is a hint). Further information on the PhD in Computer Science programme can be found on the \"PhD in Computer Science\" webpage. All applications are made online via the University's Applicant Portal; refer to the https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/courses/directory/cscspdpcs webpage. Please quote the reference NR50923 in the Research Topic field so that your application can be routed directly to Dr. Seaborn. Applications should include all of the following, without AI assistance: Academic transcripts A CV 2 academic references In the online form, similarly without AI assistance: Research Summary: A brief outline of your research proposal (like an abstract) Research Experience: An outline of your research interests and relevant background, notably describing the qualities and experience you will bring to the role; succinct but with sufficient detail to understand what you did, especially in team contexts Career Goals: And notably why a PhD would help you with these Additional Information to Support Your Application: Please describe circumstances where you faced hindrances or circumstances which mean that you cannot safely carry out work on topics like diversity in your nation of residence Course Specific Questions > Core-reasons for applying: A motivation statement covering why you wish to pursue a PhD in this area and in my lab specifically The research proposal in one PDF that includes: A ~2-page research proposal A ~1-page outline of first year deliverables (be sure to do your research on the programme) Bibliographic references on a fourth page An application is only complete when all supporting documents, including the 2 academic references, are submitted. It is your responsibility to ensure that both referees submit their references before the closing date. Applicants are expected to apply for the University Funding Competitions, but, in the event of an unsuccessful funding application, Dr. Seaborn may have the ability to award the successful candidate with a Home Rate Studentship. Note that a successful international student would need to self-fund the difference between the home and international fees. The University actively supports equality, diversity, and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. Please quote reference NR50923 on your application and in any correspondence about this studentship. Please note that applications for academic year 2027/2028 open mid-September 2026. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Please quote reference NR50923 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Key information Department/location Department of Computer Science and Technology Salary Reference NR50923 Category Studentships Date published 2 September 2026 Closing date 2 December 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/post-doctoral-research-associate-fixed-term-ma50990": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Post-Doctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Post-Doctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Post-Doctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 1 year in the first instance. Applications are invited for a post-doctoral research associate to join the group of Professor Bernardes at the Yusuf Hamied Department of Chemistry, University of Cambridge to work on a Michael J. Fox Foundation for Parkinson's Research funded project investigating Age-related RNA oxidation and ribosomal dysfunction in Parkinson's disease vulnerable dopamine neurons. The researcher will be responsible for the design, synthesis, purification, and validation of novel RNA-degrading compounds. Expertise in synthetic chemistry, biochemical assay development and live cell imaging is essential for this role. The successful candidate will have (or be about to obtain) a PhD in chemical biology. They will be highly motivated and able to work independently. Excellent organisational and interpersonal skills are required to ensure success in liaising with a large and diverse research team. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload your Curriculum Vitae (CV), a covering letter and publications list in the upload section of the online application. If you upload any additional documents that have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. For queries relating to your application or the application process, please contact Dr Sarah Pallet via email on skp41@cam.ac.uk Please quote reference MA50990 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The Department holds an Athena SWAN silver award for women in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics, and Medicine. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further information Key information Department/location Yusuf Hamied Department of Chemistry Salary £33,002 - £35,608 or £37,694- £46,049 Reference MA50990 Category Research Date published 8 September 2026 Closing date 22 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/post-doctoral-research-associate-in-experimental-quantum-optics-and-quantum-light-spectroscopy-fixed-ma50961": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Research Associate in Experimental Quantum Optics and Quantum-Light Spectroscopy (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Post-Doctoral Research Associate in Experimental Quantum Optics and Quantum-Light Spectroscopy (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Post-Doctoral Research Associate in Experimental Quantum Optics and Quantum-Light Spectroscopy (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Post-Doctoral Research Associate in Experimental Quantum Optics and Quantum-Light Spectroscopy (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 24 months in the first instance. Applications are invited for 2 Post-Doctoral Research Associates to join the group of Dr Raj Pandya in the Department of Chemistry at the University of Cambridge. The positions are each funded for 24 months through funding from UKRI. The successful candidates will undertake interdisciplinary research at the interface of physical chemistry, spectroscopy and materials science. Projects will broadly focus on developing and applying advanced ultrafast, nonlinear and quantum-enabled measurement approaches to understand complex physicochemical processes, including both light-driven and non-light-driven phenomena. Application areas may include batteries and other energy materials, electrocatalysis, functional molecular and condensed-phase systems, and the development of new experimental tools (including at free-electron lasers) for probing molecular dynamics, fluctuations, and charge or energy transport. Candidates should have (or be about to obtain) a PhD in a relevant specialist field.  Strong experimental skills in nonlinear optics and ultrafast spectroscopy are required, together with strong computational skills, including hardware control and programming, instrument automation, data acquisition and analysis. Experience in developing or integrating optical instrumentation would be advantageous.\nThe researchers will work within a collaborative and multidisciplinary environment spanning chemistry, physics and materials science, with opportunities to develop new experimental methodologies and contribute to a broad range of fundamental and applied research projects We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for part-time working or other flexible working arrangements. We particularly welcome applications from women and /or candidates from a BME background for this vacancy as they are currently under-represented at this level in our University. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload your Curriculum Vitae (CV), a covering letter and publications list in the upload section of the online application. If you upload any additional documents that have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. For queries relating to your application or the application process, please contact Dr Raj Pandya via email on rp558@cam.ac.uk . Please quote reference MA50961 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The Department holds an Athena SWAN silver award for women in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics, and Medicine. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further information Key information Department/location Yusuf Hamied Department of Chemistry Salary £34,610-£35,608 £37,694-£46,049 Reference MA50961 Category Research Date published 4 September 2026 Closing date 18 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/post-doctoral-research-associate-x-2-fixed-term-ma50962": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Research Associate x 2 (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Post-Doctoral Research Associate x 2 (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Post-Doctoral Research Associate x 2 (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Post-Doctoral Research Associate x 2 (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 1 year in the first instance.  The positions are available immediately. The Gaunt group are looking for passionate and creative scientists to join our team as postdoctoral researchers in synthetic chemistry in the Department of Chemistry at the University of Cambridge. The successful candidate will join the dynamic and collaborative research group of Professor Matthew Gaunt, working at the forefront of cutting-edge synthetic methodologies and their applications ( https://www.thegauntgroup.com/ ). Position overview - As a postdoctoral researcher, you will be involved in designing and developing catalytic activation modes that lead to new synthetic transformations. Synthetic chemists are uniquely placed to leverage catalytic systems to design reactions that control structure and function at a molecular level in all types of bioactive complex molecules. We are looking for experts in synthetic chemistry who are interested in taking on new challenges and exploiting their experience to explore new areas of catalyst-controlled reactivity that lead to an expansion of accessible chemical space.\nQualifications: We are looking for passionate and creative scientists to work as part of a collaborative team. Excellent organisational and interpersonal skills are required to ensure success in liaising with a large and diverse research team: Ph.D. (or be about to obtain) in Organic Chemistry. Strong background in synthetic organic chemistry. Demonstrated track record of high-quality research. Equal Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) Criteria - In the Gaunt Group we are committed to providing a welcoming and supportive space for all. We are a sociable group and productively work to create an environment where everyone is free to be themselves. All members are encouraged to contribute to our scientific community where the sharing of knowledge and development of ideas forms the foundations of the laboratory. We foster a diverse and inclusive environment and encourage applications from candidates of all backgrounds, experiences and identities. Our research group values and prioritises diversity, equity and inclusion, and we actively seek candidates who can contribute to these goals. Opportunities within Gaunt Group - In this post, you will also be exposed to a range of chemistry and we collaborate widely with the pharmaceutical industry and academic groups. We have an excellent structure for the continued education of group members and offer teaching and mentoring opportunities to enhance leadership skills.\nApplication Process: To apply, please submit the following documents: Cover letter detailing your research experience and interest in the position. Curriculum vitae (CV) with a list of publications. A brief research statement outlining your past research accomplishments and future research interests. Contact information for three references. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. For queries relating to your application or the application process, please contact Professor Matthew Gaunt via email on gaunt-group@ch.cam.ac.uk . Please quote reference MA50962 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The Department holds an Athena SWAN silver award for women in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics, and Medicine. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further information Key information Department/location Yusuf Hamied Department of Chemistry Salary £33,002-£46,049 Reference MA50962 Category Research Date published 4 September 2026 Closing date 2 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/post-doctoral-research-associate-nuclear-quantum-effects-in-non-equilibrium-dynamics-fixed-term-ma50960": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Research Associate: Nuclear Quantum Effects in Non-Equilibrium Dynamics (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Post-Doctoral Research Associate: Nuclear Quantum Effects in Non-Equilibrium Dynamics (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Post-Doctoral Research Associate: Nuclear Quantum Effects in Non-Equilibrium Dynamics (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Post-Doctoral Research Associate: Nuclear Quantum Effects in Non-Equilibrium Dynamics (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 1 year in the first instance. Applicants are invited to apply for a position of research associate (post-doc) in the group of Mariana Rossi. For this position, the candidate will develop methodology based on imaginary-time path-integral approximations that can be applied to different flavours of non-equilibrium nuclear dynamics. The candidate will conduct benchmarks on model systems and develop new methodology that is applicable to multi-dimensional systems. Collaborative work with other theory groups is envisaged. The implementations will be performed on an in-house code for development and on a large-scale electronic nuclear dynamics code for applications. The successful candidate will work in a lively group and an excellent research environment, with sufficient computational resources to complete the project hosted in the Chemistry department. Collaborations with other theory and experimental groups are welcome, and participation in international research conferences strongly supported. We are looking for highly qualified candidates with experience in the field of quantum dynamics simulations, molecular dynamics techniques, as well as approximations to imaginary time path integrals for dynamics. Strong mathematical background and programming expertise is necessary. The candidate should have performed studies in the fields of Physics, Chemistry or Materials Science. We will select candidates that show proficiency in maths, as well as a strong background in theoretical and computational solid-state physics, physical chemistry or chemical physics. The candidate should have finished or be about to finish their PhD degree (or equivalent). We particularly welcome applications from women and /or candidates from a BME background for this vacancy as they are currently under-represented at this level in our University. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload your Curriculum Vitae (CV), a covering letter and publications list in the upload section of the online application. If you upload any additional documents that have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. For queries relating to your application or the application process, please contact Prof Marian Rossi via email on mr2102@cam.ac.uk Please quote reference MA50960 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The Department holds an Athena SWAN silver award for women in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics, and Medicine. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further information Key information Department/location Yusuf Hamied Department of Chemistry Salary £34,610-£35,608 £37,694-£46,049 Reference MA50960 Category Research Date published 4 September 2026 Closing date 18 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/postdoctoral-research-associate-fixed-term-su51024": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate  (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate  (Fixed Term) We are seeking to appoint a Postdoctoral Research Associate (PDRA) in auditory cognitive neuroscience based at the CBU (with its transition into CMBI). The PDRA will work on a Wellcome Trust funded project led by Dr Guangting Mai studying the neural basis of audiovisual speech processing in adult cochlear implant (CI) users. Main advisors/collaborators include Drs Matt Davis and Bob Carlyon (CBU) and Prof Doug Hartley (Nottingham BRC). The project aims to study how different sensory modalities (audition and vision) work together to support day-to-day speech comprehension by CI users. We also aim to address an imminent and critical clinical question of whether processing of visual speech (talker's lip movement during face-to-face communication) is associated with adaptative or maladaptative changes in auditory processing and audiovisual integration of speech during CI rehabilitation. We will address these questions through sets of cross-sectional and longitudinal experiments in adult CI users combining sophisticated brain imaging of electroencephalography (EEG) and high-density diffuse optical tomography (HD-DOT). Findings of the project would provide important implications for clinicians to consider appropriate strategies as to how patients should be instructed to use visual speech and what their impacts may be for speech and language development during CI rehabilitation. The findings may also provide evidence to use brain imaging to better predict post-implantation outcomes and/or guidance for neuromodulation to improve CI users' speech comprehension. An ideal candidate is expected to have a strong background (PhD in neuroscience, psychology or auditory/speech sciences) and extensive experience in conducting EEG/MEG and/or functional neuroimaging experiments (fNIRS/DOT/fMRI) in human participants. Experience in working with clinical populations (e.g., participants with hearing, speech or language disorders) is highly desirable. Relevant analyses expertise (including Matlab or Python programming) will also be a strong asset. Funding for this post is available for a fixed term of 3 years full time or extended pro rata for part time (preferably 0.7 FTE). Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a health assessment, a security check and a standard Disclosure and Barring Service check. This appointment also requires a Research Passport application. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Closing Date: 16th October 2026 Interview Date: to be confirmed (expected November 2026) Please quote reference SU51024 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location MRC Cognition and Brain Sciences Unit Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference SU51024 Category Research Date published 14 September 2026 Closing date 16 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/postdoctoral-research-associate-fixed-term-x2-rr50983": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term) x2",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term) x2 | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term) x2 Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term) x2 Applications are invited for an enthusiastic and motivated x2 Postdoctoral Research Associate to join the Williams-Gray Lab for a 24-month fixed-term position, contributing to an exciting project investigating STING (Stimulator of Interferon Genes) as a potential therapeutic target for Parkinson's disease. The Williams-Gray lab works at the interface of clinical and laboratory science, aiming to better understand the role of the immune system in Parkinson's, with the goal of advancing the development disease-modifying treatments. The project will be run in collaboration with the Alborada Drug Discovery Institute, which aims to accelerate the discovery of novel therapeutics for neurogenerative disorders. The successful candidate will lead experimental research aimed at understanding the role of STING-driven inflammation in Parkinson's disease and determining its potential as a target for disease modification. Working with patient-derived monocytes, the postholder will use cellular, proteomic and transcriptomic approaches to map inflammatory signatures associated with STING activation and assess responses to STING inhibition across different stages and clinical phenotypes of Parkinson's disease. The postholder will take a leading role in designing and delivering experiments, analysing and interpreting complex datasets, and contributing to the development of the wider research programme. The work will provide an opportunity to combine mechanistic cellular biology with patient-derived models and high-dimensional molecular profiling, with the ultimate aim of identifying new approaches to stratification and treatment in Parkinson's. This is an excellent opportunity for an early-career postdoctoral researcher to develop an interdisciplinary research profile at the interface of neurodegeneration, immunology and translational medicine, while working within a collaborative research environment. We are seeking an early career researcher with a PhD in biomedical science/neuroscience and a strong interest in translational research in Parkinson's disease or neurodegeneration. Candidates should have practical experience in cell culture and experimental biology (ideally including cell stimulation assays in human PBMCs) as well as flow cytometry. Experience    in proteomic profiling, transcriptomic approaches, and analysis of complex biological datasets is highly desirable. Good organisational, analytical and communication skills, together with enthusiasm for developing as an independent researcher, are essential. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo an enhanced Disclosure and Barring Service check. This appointment also requires a Research Passport application. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. For questions relating to the post, please contact Caroline Williams-Gray at chm27@cam.ac.uk Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Closing Date: 27th September 2026 Interview Date: to be confirmed Please quote reference RR50983 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Clinical Neurosciences Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference RR50983 Category Research Date published 7 September 2026 Closing date 27 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/professorship-of-veterinary-science-pp51038": {
      "title": "Professorship of Veterinary Science",
      "description": "Professorship of Veterinary Science | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Professorship of Veterinary Science Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Professorship of Veterinary Science The Board of Electors to the Professorship of Veterinary Science invite applications for this Professorship from persons whose work is connected with veterinary science (ideally in research aligned with One Health) to take up appointment in 2027. In addition to serving as Professor of Veterinary Science, the postholder will serve as Head of Department of Veterinary Medicine (for five years in the first instance, with the option for renewal), providing strategic vision, intellectual direction, and transformational leadership for one of the world's leading centres of veterinary teaching, research, and clinical excellence. The Department of Veterinary Medicine is the Veterinary School of the University of Cambridge, the highest-rated research university in the United Kingdom, and one of the top five research universities in the world. The Department has a national and international reputation for excellence in veterinary science and education and recently ranked fourth in the world in the Top QS Universities rankings. With a renewed commitment to veterinary education from the University, the Department is entering a period of significant change and renewal, providing an opportunity for the new Head to shape the future of veterinary medicine at Cambridge and reinforce its position as a global leader in veterinary science, research and clinical innovation. Candidates will have an outstanding research record of international stature in veterinary science or a related area, together with demonstrable academic and research leadership experience, including substantial experience of leading successful research programmes, delivering high-quality teaching, and fostering excellence across research, education and ideally also clinical service environments. Candidates should also bring demonstrable experience leading, or contributing significantly to, successful external accreditation processes. The ideal candidate will bring an understanding of veterinary medicine and the evolving needs of the veterinary profession, together with the vision, ambition and enthusiasm to lead the Department through transformational change and renewal. They will hold a PhD or equivalent postgraduate qualification. Standard professorial duties include teaching and research, examining, supervision and administration. As Head of Department, the postholder will provide strategic and intellectual leadership, setting the Department's academic direction and long-term vision. They will foster a culture of excellence, innovation, collegiality and inclusive, values-driven leadership across teaching, research and clinical domains.  The Professor will be based in Cambridge.  A competitive salary will be offered. More information, including a detailed role description and person specification, and how to apply is available at: https://opportunities.mcleanpartnership.com/vetsci/ or contact Dr Graham Little at McLean Partnership on +44 (0)203 725 3757 or via email at grahamlittle@mcleanpartnership.com . Applications, consisting of a letter of application, a statement of current and future research plans, a curriculum vitae and a publications list, along with details of three referees should be sent no later than midnight on 19 October 2026. Please quote reference PP51038 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Veterinary Medicine Salary Reference PP51038 Category Professorships and Directorships Date published 10 September 2026 Closing date 19 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/project-manager-remote-sensing-digital-data-co-ordinator-south-asia-temporary-cover-jc50930": {
      "title": "Project Manager & Remote Sensing Digital Data Co-ordinator South Asia [Temporary Cover]",
      "description": "Project Manager & Remote Sensing Digital Data Co-ordinator South Asia [Temporary Cover] | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Project Manager & Remote Sensing Digital Data Co-ordinator South Asia [Temporary Cover] Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Project Manager & Remote Sensing Digital Data Co-ordinator South Asia [Temporary Cover] Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available until 31st December 2027. The McDonald Institute invites applications for a full-time, fixed term Project Manager & Remote Sensing Digital Data Co-ordinator, generously supported by Arcadia ( https://www.arcadiafund.org.uk/ ). This is a fixed term post until 31 December 2027. The post holder will be employed on the Mapping Archaeological Heritage in South Asia (MAHSA) project to provide secondment cover for the Project Manager. MAHSA is identifying and documenting endangered archaeological heritage sites across the Indus and Ganges River Basins and surrounding regions of Pakistan and northwest India using a combination of remote sensing, machine learning, historical map analysis, records-based research and selective archaeological surveys. Records of these sites are being made available in an open source Arches ( https://www.archesproject.org ) geospatial relational database tailored for different interest groups and stakeholders. Under the direction of the Principal Investigator, the role holder will provide cover for the Project Manager while she is on secondment. The role holder will be supported by the Project Manager as part of her secondment role, and there will be a handover period. Key tasks will include: managing the implementation of an international, multi-partner project aimed at using remote sensing and machine learning technologies and archive digitisation to map archaeological and other heritage sites across Pakistan and northwest India, to produce an Open Access database of this information. The role will also involve: providing the day-to-day coordination of the project and project team, maintaining appropriate administration systems and procedures to help ensure the project continues to be delivered on time, to budget, and to the required quality standards; producing detailed project reports including financial reports; organising and facilitating contributions from project partners and other stakeholders, and managing the development of protocols, training materials, documentation, and publications in line with best practice and project objectives. Additional duties include undertaking and coordinating the documentation of archaeological sites and monuments through the use of remote sensing and map analyses and desk-based research on existing sites, and ground-based verification fieldwork; and organising and overseeing project events such as conferences, workshops and training sessions. The post is likely to involve international travel to different parts of Pakistan and India if appropriate. Applicants must have a PhD/DPhil level qualification in Archaeology, Archaeological GIS or Remote Sensing with extensive post-degree work experience in heritage management and/or remote sensing analysis. Applicants should also have a proven record of international research project management and accomplishment in research using remote sensing and GIS technologies within an archaeological or heritage setting. Experience in the archaeology (or a closely related and relevant subject) of South Asia or neighbouring regions is desirable. Ability and willingness to travel (nationally and internationally) and to work flexibly to support the activities of the project is essential, and experience of using Arches is desirable. Starting date: 1 October 2026, or as soon as possible thereafter Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please do not apply without consulting the Further Particulars (see further particulars by following the link for details) as these give you essential information about the procedure and the documents you need to submit. Informal inquiries may be addressed to Prof. Cameron Petrie ( cap59@cam.ac.uk ). The closing date for applications is: Midnight on 24th September 2026 Interviews are expected to take place on 29th September 2026 If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact: hr@hsps.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference JC50930 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars of role Key information Department/location McDonald Institute for Archaeological Research Salary £47,389-£59,966 Reference JC50930 Category Academic-related Date published 10 September 2026 Closing date 24 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-assistant-fixed-term-rr50946": {
      "title": "Research Assistant (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Assistant (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant (Fixed Term) We are looking for an enthusiastic and technically skilled research assistant to join the research group of Dr. Elizabeth Radford in the Department of Clinical Neurosciences ( https://www.sanger.ac.uk/external_person/radford-elizabeth/ ). We are applying high throughput molecular and cellular techniques such as CRISPR/CAS9 mediated Saturation Genome Editing (SGE) to assess the effect of variants in genes associated with genetic neurodevelopmental conditions.  These data will allow us to more accurately estimate the impact of any genetic variant later observed in a patient in clinic.  The techniques include molecular biology and cell culture, CRISPR/CAS9 mediated recombination of large variant libraries, next-generation sequencing assays, and single-cell RNAseq.  You will work with a team to apply established protocols, and to develop novel techniques in efforts to broaden the cell types and technical approaches used in the lab.  These efforts will contribute to a large-scale international effort to generate maps of variant effect for hundreds of disease genes.  Through expert technical input into on-going and emerging projects, you will advance our understanding of genetic conditions that affect children's development. You will be based at the Wellcome Genome Campus, a highly collaborative environment that encourages creativity and scientific excellence with access to state-of-the-art research facilities and technologies. We actively translate our research into the clinic. In our lab, you will oversee and provide technical support. You will be involved in the development and implementation of new techniques and will assist with the training of other lab members. You will participate in group meetings and internal seminars. You will participate actively in experimental design and troubleshooting, have the freedom to develop your own research questions and experiments, and be encouraged to take up training opportunities. We have a strong commitment to both personal and professional development. You will be encouraged to develop your own ideas and build your research profile. You will demonstrate core values of enthusiasm, openness, kindness and integrity, and will join a dynamic and highly collaborative group. Working in the Radford Lab offers a unique opportunity to be immersed in a vibrant, multidisciplinary research environment. Located within the Wellcome Sanger Institute on the Wellcome Genome Campus, the lab benefits from close interactions with: The Wellcome Sanger Institute: This provides access to a highly collaborative multi-disciplinary genomics research environment equipped with exceptional experimental and computational infrastructure, world-class training and professional development opportunities. European Bioinformatics Institute (EMBL-EBI): Co-located on the Wellcome Genome Campus, EMBL-EBI is an international leader in data science, and provides access to a joint translational ecosystem, further training and professional development opportunities. The Department of Clinical Neurosciences: The Radford Lab is part of the Department of Clinical Neurosciences, providing access to cutting-edge clinical research and fostering collaborations with neurologists and other pre-clinical researchers. This proximity allows a seamless connection to the clinical aspects of the field and to translate research findings into patient care. The Clinical School: Offers exposure to teaching and learning opportunities, with potential for involvement in educational activities and interaction with students and faculty (more information is reported below). UK Dementia Research Institute (UK DRI): Allows interaction with researchers at the forefront of dementia research, with opportunities for the exploration of shared genetic architecture between rare and common neurological and neurodegenerative diseases, contributing to the development of new treatments and preventative strategies. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. For questions relating to the post, please contact Elizabeth Radford at ejr41@cam.ac.uk Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Closing Date: 1st October 2026 Interview Date: to be confirmed Mid October Please quote reference RR50946 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Clinical Neurosciences Salary £33,002-£35,608 Reference RR50946 Category Research Date published 3 September 2026 Closing date 1 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-assistant-fixed-term-ps50966": {
      "title": "Research Assistant (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Assistant (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant (Fixed Term) Bioprinting New Alternative Methodologies (NAMs) Recent breakthroughs in stem cell biology, organoid culture and bioengineering provide unprecedented opportunities to recreate complex aspects of human biology in a dish. However, current three-dimensional culture systems often lack the spatial organisation, cellular complexity and tissue architecture required to faithfully recapitulate human physiology. Here, we aim to harness next-generation bioprinting to develop clinically relevant New Alternative Methodologies (NAMs) for modelling human biology in vitro. The Boroviak Lab are looking for a highly motivated Research Assistant to support our bioprinting efforts at the Cambridge Stem Cell Institute. The project aims to combine stem cell biology, tissue culture and bioengineering with state-of-the-art bioprinting to generate physiologically relevant three-dimensional models of human tissues. The successful candidate will work closely with members of the Boroviak Lab and our collaborators across the Stem Cell Institute to establish and characterise new bioprinted tissue models. This is an exciting opportunity to work at the interface of stem cell biology and bioengineering, with the opportunity to develop new approaches to study human disease in vitro. The postholder will actively contribute to our research and be responsible for the establishment, optimisation and characterisation of bioprinted tissue models. The candidate will have an excellent background in biology and previous laboratory experience. Experience with mammalian tissue culture and particularly stem cell or organoid culture would be ideal. Previous experience in bioengineering, biomaterials, three-dimensional culture or bioprinting would be advantageous, but is not essential. Most importantly, we are looking for a highly motivated, curious and collaborative individual who is keen to learn new approaches and develop innovative experimental strategies. The successful candidate will be expected to work efficiently with researchers from different scientific backgrounds and contribute positively to the collaborative research environment of the Boroviak Lab and Cambridge Stem Cell Institute. While this is primarily a research position, the post also entails maintaining the infrastructure required for our bioprinting activities, preparation and maintenance of cell cultures, ordering of equipment and reagents, training students and visiting researchers in techniques and equipment use, as well as instructing correct safety procedures. The Cambridge Stem Cell Institute provides a state-of-the-art and highly collaborative environment for stem cell biology. The Boroviak Lab has established collaborations with research groups across the Institute and Cambridge to develop bioprinted NAMs that reproduce complex aspects of human biology in a dish. The postholder will actively contribute to these collaborative research efforts and help establish bioprinting approaches that can be adapted to different biological questions and tissue systems. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 1 years from the start date. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. To apply for this post, please submit\n (1) a CV and motivation letter detailing why you want to be part of this project and how this post will be a good stepping-stone for your career and (2) the contact details for two people who can provide a professional reference. In addition, it is essential that applicant submits (3) a two-page research outline describing how they would use bioprinting to develop a clinically relevant New Alternative Methodology. The proposal should clearly state the biological or clinical rationale, describe how the proposed model would work experimentally and explain how bioprinting would provide an advantage over existing approaches. The research outline should include illustrations of the proposed model and describe the potential experimental outcomes and their significance. Applications without this two-page research outline will not be considered. The closing date for applications is: 23rd September 2026. The interview date for the role is: To be confirmed Please quote reference PS50966 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Cambridge Stem Cell Institute (SBS) Salary £33,002-£35,608 Reference PS50966 Category Research Date published 4 September 2026 Closing date 23 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
