    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/senior-communications-engagement-and-events-manager-ka51004": {
      "title": "Senior Communications, Engagement and Events Manager",
      "description": "Senior Communications, Engagement and Events Manager | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Senior Communications, Engagement and Events Manager Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Senior Communications, Engagement and Events Manager Could you be our next Senior Communications, Engagement and Events Manager? The Cavendish Laboratory is seeking an experienced communications leader to head up our communications, events, and outreach function. Based in one of the world's most respected physics departments, this senior role offers scope to shape how we engage with our audiences at a pivotal moment of our development. As a member of the Department's Senior Leadership Team, reporting to the Head of Operations, you will set the strategic direction for how we communicate across every channel and audience: from colleagues and students to alumni, industry partners, and the wider public. You will connect people to the science being done here and lead the outreach work that inspires the next generation of physicists. You will lead a small team of managers and coordinators, providing clear direction, developing individual capability, and fostering a collaborative, open and supportive working environment. You will oversee the department's communications budget and take responsibility for the team's output across all areas of activity. The scope of that activity is broad. Your work will span digital and print communications, social media, our website, a programme of events from public lectures to high-profile stakeholder gatherings, alumni and supporter publications, and an outreach programme reaching young people from Key Stage 2 to pre-university age. You will also act as an internal expert, advising senior colleagues on communications and engagement strategy, and building relationships with the University's central communications teams to coordinate joint campaigns and maximise impact. We are looking for someone with: Experience developing and delivering multi-channel communications and engagement strategies across diverse audiences and with multiple partners. A track record of communicating scientific or technical subjects compellingly to non-specialist audiences. Strong leadership and line management skills, with a proven ability to motivate and develop a team. Experience working in complex stakeholder environments and across organisational boundaries. Excellent editorial, writing, and proofreading skills, with rigorous attention to quality and brand. Good working knowledge of social media platforms and digital analytics tools. A relevant degree or equivalent professional experience. A background in science or science communication is desirable. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be directed to Vanessa Bismuth at vanessa.bismuth@phy.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference KA51004 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Senior Communications Manager further particulars Key information Department/location Department of Physics Salary £42,254-£56,535 Reference KA51004 Category Academic-related Date published 9 September 2026 Closing date 30 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/senior-research-associate-xz51036": {
      "title": "Senior Research Associate",
      "description": "Senior Research Associate | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Senior Research Associate Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Senior Research Associate We seek an experienced and highly motivated Senior Research Associate to join the Milner Therapeutics Institute (MTI) at the University of Cambridge, based at the Jeffrey Cheah Biomedical Centre on the Cambridge Biomedical Campus. As a Senior Research Associate, you will provide scientific and operational leadership for the UK-based team for the UK-ROK Collaboration on Engineering Biology and AI for Gene and Cell Therapy, an international research programme funded until March 2029. You will work closely with the Group Leader and Head of Research to coordinate collaborative research across the University of Cambridge, Korean research organisations, and industry and clinical partners. The successful candidate will hold a PhD in a relevant specialist subject and have substantial postdoctoral experience as well as experience in AI, computational biology, bioinformatics, engineering biology, gene or cell therapy, or a closely related field relevant to the programme. The University of Cambridge actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and welcomes applications from all sections of society. The University is committed to fair treatment throughout the recruitment process and will make reasonable adjustments to enable disabled applicants to compete to the best of their ability. Informal enquiries about the role are welcomed and should be directed to Namshik Han, Head of Computational Biology and Artificial Intelligence, at nh417@cam.ac.uk . Queries regarding the application process should be directed to Ella May Bailey, HR and Administration Coordinator, at hr@milner.cam.ac.uk . Closing date: 27 September 2026. Interview date: TBC. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please quote reference XZ51036 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information FPs Key information Department/location Milner Therapeutics Institute Salary £42,254-£56,535 Reference XZ51036 Category Research Date published 11 September 2026 Closing date 27 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/service-technician-aq51010": {
      "title": "Service Technician",
      "description": "Service Technician | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Service Technician Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Service Technician An opportunity has arisen for motivated Service Technician to join our friendly Biofacility team within Cambridge. The Service Technician maintains the essential provision of clean cages, drinking water bottles and materials to meet requirements of staff and researchers. After initial training you will be able to operate simple machinery, in order to prepare, clean, sterilise and maintain animal caging and associated equipment, including dismantling and reassembling equipment for washing and sterilisation. You will be responsible for the cleaning of cages and equipment, alongside tasks involved with ensuring adequate stocks of clean caging, equipment and consumable items to meet the daily needs of the facility. You will also support the technicians in other duties including general cleaning of the facility, cleaning and replenishment of Personal Protective Equipment. You will also be involved in the collection and delivery of stores, maintaining stocks and disposal of waste (including clinical waste). This role is physically demanding requiring regular and repetitive movements including lifting, carrying and manual dexterity for which training will be given. You will be a team player with good communication skills; literate, numerate and basic IT literacy are important. You will have good attention to detail, able to work in a clean and organised way, able to manage your own time and workload. Previous experience working with industrial cagewash / autoclave equipment or in a biomedical animal facility are also desirable. In addition to the base salary stated above, the successful candidate for this post will receive an additional 2.5% supplement to their pay. The post is available immediately and subject to the satisfactory completion of a 3 month probationary period. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a health assessment and a security check. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be directed to UBSRecruitment@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference AQ51010 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Salary £25,249 Reference AQ51010 Category Assistant staff Date published 11 September 2026 Closing date 2 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/service-technician-barrier-aq51063": {
      "title": "Service Technician",
      "description": "Service Technician - Barrier | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Service Technician - Barrier Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Service Technician - Barrier An opportunity has arisen for a Service Technician (Barrier) to join our friendly team within a busy Biofacility in South Cambridge. The Service Technician (Barrier) provides a valuable service to ensure the smooth operation of the biomedical barrier and service areas within the facility with the essential provision of clean cages and equipment, consumables and other stock to meet requirements of staff and researchers. After initial training you will be able to operate simple machinery in order to prepare, clean, sterilise and maintain animal caging and associated equipment. The role holder will complete general portering duties, primarily the internal distribution of caging, equipment and scrubs to meet research requirements. The role holder will also complete general cleaning of procedural areas, replenishment of PPE and gowns, and maintaining a high standard of cleanliness throughout the barrier. The role holder shall ensure the correct storage and disposal of all waste materials including clinical waste. \nThis role is physically demanding requiring regular and repetitive movements including lifting, carrying and manual dexterity for which training will be given. You will be a team player with good communication skills; be literate, numerate with basic IT skills. You will have good attention to detail, able to work in a clean and organised way, able to manage your own time and workload. Previous experience working with industrial cagewash / autoclave equipment, and an understanding of relevant Home Office regulations would be an advantage. The post is available immediately and subject to the satisfactory completion of a 3 month probationary period. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a health assessment and a security check. Please quote reference AQ51063 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Salary £25,249 Reference AQ51063 Category Assistant staff Date published 14 September 2026 Closing date 5 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/supervisory-service-technician-internal-applicants-only-aq51009": {
      "title": "Supervisory Service Technician [Internal Applicants only]",
      "description": "Supervisory Service Technician [Internal Applicants only] | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Supervisory Service Technician [Internal Applicants only] Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Supervisory Service Technician [Internal Applicants only] An opportunity has arisen for motivated Supervisory Service Technician to join our friendly team within a busy biofacility in Cambridge. The Supervisory Service Technician will ensure the smooth operation of the service area within the facility. The role holder will be responsible for the daily cleaning, movement and preparation of clean caging, consumables, stock, and other equipment through use of cagewash, bottle wash and other associated machinery to meet the daily requirements of staff, and research users in the facility. The role holder shall also complete general cleaning and maintenance duties within the cagewash and storage area including ensuring the correct storage and disposal of all waste materials including clinical waste. The Supervisory Service Technician will act as a key point of contact for the Service team, communicating with the other biofacility staff regarding caging and equipment requirements, providing basic supervision of the service team including allocation of daily tasks and prioritising when required. The role holder will be required to provide a high standard of work, organisation and hygiene as directed by departmental Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs). The role will involve lifting and manual handling for which training will be given, as well regular repetitive and physically demanding tasks. The successful candidate will be a motivated team player with good communication skills, literate, numerate and basic IT literacy are important. You will have good attention to detail, able to work in a clean and organised way, able to manage your own time and workload. Previous experience working with industrial cage wash / autoclave equipment within an academic or commercial laboratory is essential, although experience working within a similar designated biomedical facility would be an advantage. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a health assessment and a security check. The University will determine whether an outcome is satisfactory. In addition to the base salary stated above, the successful candidate for this post will receive an additional 2.5% supplement to their pay. The role is available immediately and is subject to completion of a 3-month probationary period. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a health assessment and a security check. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be directed to UBSRecruitment@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference AQ51009 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Salary £25,249-£27,319 Reference AQ51009 Category Assistant staff Date published 11 September 2026 Closing date 25 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/technical-assistant-part-time-fixed-term-pm51054": {
      "title": "Technical Assistant (Part Time, Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Technical Assistant (Part Time, Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Technical Assistant (Part Time, Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Technical Assistant (Part Time, Fixed Term) The Niakan Lab at University of Cambridge is seeking a technical assistant to join our team. This post will support pioneering research projects to provide fundamental insights into human biology and reproduction. The primary responsibility of this position is to drive to collect bovine ovaries from an abattoir and bring them back to our laboratory in Cambridge (rental car will be provided). The postholder will dissect discarded animal tissue at the abattoir and place them in a collection container to bring back to our laboratory in Cambridge. The candidate must be able to work independently and follow a strict schedule. They will have a good level of oral communication, a high standard of English, and the ability to give and receive written and verbal instructions. A high attention to detail and reliability is a must. The successful candidate will have a good general education or equivalent level of practical experience and will require a valid driver's license. All training will be provided on the job and it is a great opportunity to learn skills in a scientific laboratory. This post is part time, fixed term for 6 months. The hours of work will be 9:00am - 12:30pm on Tuesdays and Wednesdays each week. For Informal enquiries, please contact niakan-lab-manager@pdn.cam.ac.uk . For queries about the application process please contact pdnhr@pdn.cam.ac.uk . The University is an accredited Real Living Wage (RLW) employer, and will ensure that employees are paid at least the prevailing RLW rate. For further information, and the current rate, please visit the Living Wage webpage https://www.hr.admin.cam.ac.uk/pay-benefits/pay/living-wage . Apply: Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. On the online form, in the suitability for the role section, you should explain the reasons for your application and how you match the requirements of the role. The full contact details for two professional referees should be provided; please note that we will assume that you are happy for us to approach your referees at any stage, unless you indicate otherwise in the space provided on the form. Please quote reference PM51054 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Physiology, Development and Neuroscience Salary £23,350 pro rata Reference PM51054 Category Assistant staff Date published 11 September 2026 Closing date 30 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/unestablished-senior-occupational-health-adviser-as50931": {
      "title": "Unestablished Senior Occupational Health Adviser",
      "description": "Unestablished Senior Occupational Health Adviser | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Unestablished Senior Occupational Health Adviser Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Unestablished Senior Occupational Health Adviser Are you looking for a rewarding and fulfilling role in a supportive and inclusive working environment? The University of Cambridge is inviting applications for the position of Senior Occupational Health Adviser to join our busy, friendly team at the Occupational Health Service. This is an exciting opportunity for an experienced Registered General Nurse with the Nursing and Midwifery Council (NMC) in the adult branch, who holds a post registration qualification in Occupational Health at Certificate, Diploma or Degree level. The successful applicant will enable efficient and effective service delivery through undertaking clinical activities, such as managing health screening and surveillance programmes, and providing health advice to service users; management of junior clinical staff; and contributing to the review, development and implementation of the Service's strategies for prevention of ill health and promotion of wellbeing of service users. You should have excellent organisational and communication skills, including being proficient in use of Microsoft office programmes and databases. You will be able to work autonomously as well as part of a team.  You should also have a comprehensive working knowledge of Occupational Health Law and Health, Safety and Employment Law; experience of management of caseloads, health surveillance and health screening programmes; and be able to work in a culturally sensitive way, using your own initiative while knowing when to consult with senior colleagues. The role is full time (37 hours per week), working over five days Monday ¿ Friday. Due to the nature of this role, the post holder is required to work on-site for their full contracted hours. Please see the further information link below for a full job description, person specification and more information relating to this post. Your application should clearly set out how you meet the criteria listed in the person specification (the skills and experience required for the role) along with relevant examples. Candidates will be shortlisted based on these criteria. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a Disclosure and Barring Service check (enhanced with adults' barred list). This appointment also requires NMC Registration. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be directed to: OccHealth@admin.cam.ac.uk . Please quote reference AS50931 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Occupational Health and Safety Service Salary £42,254-£56,535 Reference AS50931 Category Academic-related Date published 9 September 2026 Closing date 7 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/workstream-lead-financial-systems-subsidiaries-and-fccs-ag50916": {
      "title": "Workstream Lead",
      "description": "Workstream Lead - Financial Systems (Subsidiaries and FCCS) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Workstream Lead - Financial Systems (Subsidiaries and FCCS) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Workstream Lead - Financial Systems (Subsidiaries and FCCS) The University is undertaking an ambitious Finance Transformation Programme, delivering major improvements to financial systems, reporting, planning, and data management across the institution. We are seeking an experienced senior specialist with strong knowledge in financial reporting and systems to play a key role in shaping the future of financial reporting and financial data governance within the University including how financial consolidation, reporting, and subsidiary operations are designed, implemented and embedded across a diverse and complex group structure. The Role Working with a high degree of autonomy, the role works closely with the Finance Transformation Programme Board, Central and Research Accounting (CRA), Financial Planning and Performance (FPP), subsidiaries, and wider University stakeholders, acting as the subject matter expert and interface between the teams and working to resolve complex issues to meet reporting requirements. The Workstream Lead is a senior professional role responsible for leading two critical strands of the financial transformation programme: (1) deployment of the new financial system across subsidiary entities, and (2) implementation of Oracle Fusion Financial Consolidation and Close (FCCS). The role is responsible for ensuring the system design meets the business requirements and ensure a successful delivery of the reporting requirements post go live for FY 2027. Your Skills The successful candidate will bring strong leadership skills and experience of financial reporting, financial reporting systems, financial data management, and enterprise performance management solutions such as Oracle EPM FCCS or similar platforms. You will be comfortable working in a complex transformation environment, managing stakeholders, leading teams, and driving successful delivery across multiple workstreams. This is an excellent opportunity to join a forward-thinking institution at a pivotal stage of transformation, where your expertise will directly influence the future of financial reporting and data management across the University. Benefits The University offers an excellent benefit package including: 41 days annual leave, inclusive of Bank Holidays Defined benefits pension schemes Flexible working options Family-friendly initiatives Career development opportunities Support for health and mental wellbeing Discounts on shopping Rental deposit scheme Public transport season ticket loans Tax-efficient bicycle and charity-giving schemes Location: The University is supportive of hybrid working, where some work is undertaken on University premises and some in a remote working environment. The aim of our approach is to enable as many staff as possible to work in a hybrid way if they wish and where their role allows. This arrangement is subject to change and will not form a part of the contractual terms of the role. Funding for this post is available for 31 December 2028. We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for part-time working or other flexible working arrangements. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure (criminal records check) check, a health assessment and a security check. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you would like more information about this role, informal enquiries can be directed by email to Leanne Fisher, Head of Central Accounting and Group Reporting via Leanne.Fisher@admin.cam.ac.uk If you have any questions the application process, please contact a Recruitment Administrator at Finhr_recruitment@admin.cam.ac.uk . The closing date for applications is 20 September 2026. The interviews date for the role are planned 28 September 2026. Please quote reference AG50916 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information - Workstream Lead - Subsidaries and FCCS - PDF Key information Department/location Finance Division Salary £47,389-£59,966 Reference AG50916 Category Academic-related Date published 7 September 2026 Closing date 20 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/assistant-teaching-professor-in-control-systems-nm50891": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor in Control Systems",
      "description": "Assistant Teaching Professor in Control Systems | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Assistant Teaching Professor in Control Systems Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Assistant Teaching Professor in Control Systems Applications are invited from suitably qualified candidates for the post of Assistant Teaching Professor in Control Systems. The successful candidate will join the Engineering Department's Information Engineering Division to deliver teaching at undergraduate and MEng levels in core topics related to Control Systems and Information Engineering. The successful candidate will work with academic colleagues in the Control Group and Information Engineering Division to provide teaching including: lecturing of Part IIB control courses, setting examination questions, marking of coursework and examinations, acting as module and lab leader (3rd and 4th years), leading a third year project, and assistance with curriculum revision for new Tripos. The successful candidate will be expected to have a high-level knowledge of control systems with research experience.  Previous relevant teaching experience will be an advantage. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 12 months in the first instance. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. We particularly welcome applications from women and /or candidates from a BME background for this vacancy as they are currently under-represented at this level in our department. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. In addition to this, please ensure that you upload the application documentation as follows: a curriculum vitae (CV); a statement which covers your professional and teaching experience and describes your suitability for this post (no more than 1000 words in total); and a cover letter. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. The closing date for applications is 29 September 2026. If you have any questions about the application process, please contact the HR Office ( hr-office@eng.cam.ac.uk , +44 (0) 1223 332615. Informal enquiries may be made to Professor Malcolm Smith ( mcs10@cam.ac.uk ) or Professor Ioannis Lestas ( icl20@cam.ac.uk ). Please quote reference NM50891 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Engineering Salary £47,389-£59,966 Reference NM50891 Category Academic Date published 15 September 2026 Closing date 29 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/cryo-em-industrial-projects-scientist-ph51060": {
      "title": "Cryo-EM Industrial Projects Scientist",
      "description": "Cryo-EM Industrial Projects Scientist | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Cryo-EM Industrial Projects Scientist Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Cryo-EM Industrial Projects Scientist The Department of Biochemistry is one of the largest departments within the School of the Biological Sciences, and it conducts diverse cutting-edge research on cancer, cell, structural biology, and biomolecular interactions. The Department hosts a state-of-the-art Cryo-Electron Microscopy (Cryo-EM) Facility, including Talos Arctica 200 kV and Titan Krios 300 kV electron microscopes, along with associated equipment for sample preparation, data processing, and analysis. We are seeking an enthusiastic and highly skilled Cryo-EM Industrial Projects Scientist to join the facility team. The role combines hands-on cryo-electron microscopy with the coordination and delivery of projects for industrial and commercial partners. The successful candidate will support industrial and commercial projects from initial discussions and experimental design through to data collection, analysis and completion. They will act as a key contact for external clients, provide specialist scientific advice, coordinate project activity across the facility, and help ensure work is delivered to agreed quality, timescale and service requirements. The role will also contribute to facility operations, project costings, resource planning and the continued development of industrial services. Applicants should hold a doctoral degree in a relevant scientific discipline and have strong practical experience in biological cryo-electron microscopy and structural biology analysis. Experience of working within an industrial or commercial organisation is essential, together with a strong understanding of the expectations, timelines and client-facing requirements associated with commercial scientific projects. Excellent project management, organisational, analytical and communication skills are also essential, as is the ability to work effectively with both scientific and non-scientific stakeholders. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 1 years in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Interviews are scheduled to take place between 5th - 7th October 2026. Informal discussions are welcome; please contact Dr Dimitri Chirgadze via email: dyc21@cam.ac.uk For any further assistance with the application process, please contact the Biochemistry HR team via email: personnel@bioc.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference PH51060 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information FPs_Cryo-EM-Industrial-Projects-Scientist Key information Department/location Department of Biochemistry Salary £42,254-£56,535 Reference PH51060 Category Academic-related Date published 15 September 2026 Closing date 30 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/finance-coordinator-lj51075": {
      "title": "Finance Coordinator",
      "description": "Finance Coordinator | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Finance Coordinator Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Finance Coordinator We are looking for an experienced and proactive Finance Coordinator to lead our departmental finance function and provide strategic financial leadership within a thriving academic environment. In this varied role, you will oversee budgeting, forecasting, financial reporting, compliance and financial controls, while providing expert advice to academic and professional services colleagues, researchers and senior departmental leaders. You will play a key role in supporting research funding, research facilities, studentships and income-generating activities, ensuring resources are managed effectively and sustainably. You will also lead and develop a dedicated finance team, drive process improvements, support the implementation of new financial systems and contribute to the Department's long-term planning through high-quality financial analysis and insight. This is an excellent opportunity for a finance professional with strong leadership, analytical and relationship-building skills who is keen to make a meaningful contribution within a collaborative and intellectually stimulating environment. Join us and help support exceptional teaching, world-leading research and academic excellence. For the full job description and person specification, please refer to the linked Further Particulars. The department is open to applicants who wish to work flexibly, specific arrangements can be discussed at interview. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. We encourage early applications, as the vacancy may close before the advertised deadline if a sufficient number of suitable applications are received. Informal enquiries about the role are welcomed and should be directed to Dr. Jeanne Estabel: dept-administrator@msm.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference LJ51075 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Materials Science and Metallurgy Salary £35,608-£46,049 Reference LJ51075 Category Academic-related Date published 15 September 2026 Closing date 14 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
