overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Clinical Radiologist (Part Time) An opportunity has arisen for a part-time (1 day per week, 0.2 FTE) Clinical Radiologist to join the Diagnostic Imaging service team at the Queen's Veterinary School Hospital, Cambridge. We are a large, multidisciplinary small animal referral hospital, sitting within the University of Cambridge Department of Veterinary Medicine. The Hospital operates as a commercially competitive hospital, whilst remaining central to supporting teaching of veterinary undergraduate and postgraduate students, as well as delivering and supporting veterinary clinical and one health research. The QVSH also works collaboratively with our small animal and equine primary care practices and is supported by our in-house Clinical Pathology and Anatomical Pathology services. The Role You will be responsible for providing small animal diagnostic imaging services to the Queen's Veterinary School Hospital, along with intermittent imaging support to the QVSH General Practice and Cambridge Equine Clinic, our first opinion small animal and equine services. You will actively partake in the imaging service provision including primary case management. You will be involved in the training and supervision of postgraduate Clinical Training Scholars (interns and residents) as well as being involved in on-case practical teaching of diagnostic imaging to veterinary undergraduates on their final year rotations. You may be required to partake in a small amount of diagnostic imaging lecture and practical provision to veterinary undergraduates as well as examining duties. You will assist the Principal Clinical Radiologist with marketing of the diagnostic imaging service and engagement with local referring practitioners. You will work collegiately with the wider hospital to ensure efficient patient flow through the hospital services. You will be required to participate in the imaging out of hours (OOH) rota. This will involve both primary (sole on call) for the service, as well as times offering support for imaging training scholars. When on call, either as primary contact or training scholar support, all staff members are required to be available to attend on site if needed. OOH Rota participation attracts supplementary remuneration. Further particulars for the role and information about the Department are available: https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/term/Department-of-Veterinary-Medicine If you are viewing this advert on an external site, please visit the University's job pages for access. What We Offer A supportive and engaging working environment within a collaborative multidisciplinary team. A generous annual leave entitlement provided on a pro rata basis for part-time staff, equivalent to 41 days per year (including public holidays) for a full-time employee. For this 0.2 FTE role (1 day per week), annual leave entitlement is 8 days per annual leave year. A competitive benefits package, including a range of family-friendly policies and support services. Access to staff discounts on shopping, healthcare, financial services and public transport. Membership of a generous pension scheme. An annual Continuing Professional Development (CPD) allowance. Access to tax-efficient bicycle purchase, car lease and charity-giving schemes. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. The closing date for applications is midnight on 30 September 2026. Please quote reference PP51007 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. Applications will be monitored regularly, and we may contact candidates prior to the closing date. We reserve the right to close this vacancy early if we receive a sufficient number of applications or extend the closing date if necessary. Therefore, if you are interested, please submit your application as early as possible. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information FURTHER PARTICULARS - Clinical Radiologist (Part-time, 0.2FTE) Key information Department/location Department of Veterinary Medicine Salary £47,389-£59,966 pro rata Reference PP51007 Category Academic-related Date published 16 September 2026 Closing date 30 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/deputy-business-and-operations-manager-education-lj51089": {
      "title": "Deputy Business and Operations Manager (Education)",
      "description": "Deputy Business and Operations Manager (Education) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Deputy Business and Operations Manager (Education) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Deputy Business and Operations Manager (Education) Join the Department of Materials Science & Metallurgy at the University of Cambridge and help shape an outstanding experience for postgraduate students while supporting world-leading research and education. This is a key leadership role with a strong focus on postgraduate administration, admissions, student support, governance, and service improvement. Working closely with academic and professional services colleagues, you will lead education administration, drive operational excellence, and deputise for the Business and Operations Manager when needed. Key Responsibilities\n-Lead the day-to-day delivery of education administration across the Department.\n-Manage and deliver postgraduate administration and operational processes, including admissions, recruitment, funding, and student support, ensuring high-quality services for students and academic staff throughout the student lifecycle.\n-Provide guidance to prospective postgraduate students and respond to applicant enquiries.\n-Oversee postgraduate scholarship and research funding administration.\n-Support Academic Directors and committees in implementing strategic and operational priorities.\n-Lead governance, committee management, regulatory compliance, reporting, and education data management.\n-Coordinate and enhance administrative processes to improve service quality, efficiency, and effectiveness.\n-Contribute to leadership, operational management, and continuous service improvement initiatives across the Professional Services team. You will bring experience in higher education administration, people management, budgeting, governance, and delivering complex administrative services. You will be an organised, proactive leader who thrives in a collaborative environment and is committed to excellent service delivery. This is an exciting opportunity to make a significant contribution to a dynamic academic department, supporting excellence in education while helping enable world-leading research. For a full list of job responsibilities and the person specification, please read the linked Further Particulars. We welcome applications from individuals who wish to work flexibly, specific arrangements can be discussed at interview. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. We encourage early applications, as the vacancy may close before the advertised deadline if a sufficient number of suitable applications are received. Informal enquiries about the role are welcomed and should be directed to Dr. Jeanne Estabel: dept-administrator@msm.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference LJ51089 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Materials Science and Metallurgy Salary £42,254-£56,535 Reference LJ51089 Category Academic-related Date published 16 September 2026 Closing date 14 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/director-of-the-school-of-the-biological-sciences-microscopy-bioscience-platform-associate-research-pm51041": {
      "title": "Director of the School of the Biological Sciences Microscopy Bioscience Platform (Associate Research Professor (G10)/Research Professor (G11))",
      "description": "Director of the School of the Biological Sciences Microscopy Bioscience Platform (Associate Research Professor (G10)/Research Professor (G11)) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Director of the School of the Biological Sciences Microscopy Bioscience Platform (Associate Research Professor (G10)/Research Professor (G11)) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Director of the School of the Biological Sciences Microscopy Bioscience Platform (Associate Research Professor (G10)/Research Professor (G11)) We are seeking a new Director for the Microscopy Bioscience Platform ( https://www.bio.cam.ac.uk/microscopy-platform ), based within the School of the Biological Sciences city centre campus. This strategically important position will manage, develop and inspire an established team that delivers cutting-edge approaches and supports more routine imaging techniques. Through wide-ranging collaborations with our world-leading scientists, who use microscopy in a huge range of modalities and subject areas, the chosen candidate will have unique opportunities to foster research that contributes to our reputation as a global centre of biological sciences research. In addition to the knowledge and skills to drive innovation and support our world-leading research the successful candidate will have proven leadership skills and demonstratable experience in people and facility management. Building on a foundation of innovative microscopy, the Microscopy Bioscience Platform was successfully launched in 2024 and has received substantial investment in staff and instrumentation from both internal and external funders (including successful bids to UKRI equipment calls). The role holder will capitalise on this existing commitment, ensuring that the Platform is at the forefront of microscopy and that it is accessed by a diverse portfolio of academic, industry and commercial users to secure financial sustainability. To achieve this, they will establish and maintain wide-ranging contacts across the user groups, to be familiar with their needs and future plans, as well as networking with the national and international microscopy community to keep at the forefront of the field and seize opportunities for partnerships. There would also be the opportunity to embed a research group funded from external grant applications for those who wished to further advance a relevant field. Appointment at the Associate Research Professor (G10) or Research Professor (G11) level will be dependent on experience. For Grade 11 evidence of strategic leadership, responsibility for major research programmes, management of major budgets and resources, leadership of external networks, and a broader impact through consultancy and commercial activities would be expected. The Microscopy Bioscience Platform The Microscopy Bioscience Platform is an initiative to provide a focus for microscopy support and development across seven Departments in the School of the Biological Sciences. The primary aim of the Platform is to foster innovation and collaboration, offering supported access to a wide array of advanced instruments and a focal point for technology development in the biological sciences, delivered via the Cambridge Advanced Imaging Centre (CAIC) and externally funded research grants. Across light, advanced and electron microscopy, the Platform has a substantial user group of over 180 research groups and commercial companies. The Platform operates across an extremely diverse range of science, encompassing a wide breadth of organisms and microscopy modalities. This diversity requires an agile response, based on the experience and skills of senior team members, and an excellent instrument portfolio that has been subject to significant investment from both internal and external sources of funding. This is in addition to CAIC's bespoke microscope construction that enables both development of new research approaches and technical translation to commercial applications. In fulfilling their role, the Director will be guided by an established strategic board, and their post will be managed by the Head of the Department of Physiology, Development and Neuroscience. For more information about the role and list of essential skills and experience, please refer to the Further Particulars. Informal enquiries about the position can be made to Professor Sarah Bray sjb32@cam.ac.uk or Dr Ben Steventon bjs57@cam.ac.uk . Apply: Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Applications should consist of a letter of application, a research and operational vision for developing the Microscopy Bioscience Platform (2 pages maximum) and a curriculum vitae. The full contact details for three professional referees should be provided. Please note that we will assume that you are happy for us to approach your referees at any stage, unless you indicate otherwise in the space provided on the form. The closing date for applications is 11 October 2026. Interviews are expected to be held in November. Please quote reference PM51041 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Physiology, Development and Neuroscience Salary £63,606-£71,566 Reference PM51041 Category Research Date published 11 September 2026 Closing date 11 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/facilities-assistant-ba49676-0": {
      "title": "Facilities Assistant",
      "description": "Facilities Assistant | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Facilities Assistant Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Facilities Assistant The Role: As part of Workplace Services, the Facilities Assistant is a pivotal role in supporting the operational delivery of multiple managed buildings, sites and central stores. The areas covered have an extensive range of users and provide, for example, facilities for lectures, meeting and events as well as a wide variety of office accommodation. This role is part of the operational team that support the operational delivery of excellent customer service for all users. This role must adhere to best practise in housekeeping and health and safety compliance. The Facilities Assistant reports to the Facilities Manager/Site Supervisor on operational considerations that affect maintenance of the building, Health and Safety compliance, property contracts for the third-party operators and operational staffing. Key Responsibilities: The role ensures that there is professional, smooth and joined up service delivery across all sites and buildings. The role holder works closely with the AV Team, Stores Assistants, Receptionists and Reprographics team as required on projects that cross the different disciplines. The role is responsible for soft facilities operations, maintenance of the buildings and dealing with the operational requests from the third-party occupiers and working with our building maintenance teams for reactive and planned maintenance. The role-holder is responsible for carrying out Health and Safety, Compliance, ensuring daily duties are completed, and buildings and sites are operating efficiently and effectively. About you: In order to succeed in this role, you will need the following skills: Experience is facilities, custodian or a related role customer facing Be able to use outlook and emails Collaborative working approach and team player. Good communication and interpersonal skills to interact with staff and visitors at all levels. Ability and physical fitness to perform physical tasks daily. Good time management, organisational and co-ordination skills. Basic D.I.Y skills Benefits of working at the University of Cambridge include: Competitive pay with automatic annual cost of living increases and service-related progression. Generous annual leave entitlement, helping you maintain a healthy work-life balance. Flexible working arrangements to support your lifestyle and wellbeing. Family-friendly policies, including enhanced maternity, adoption, and shared parental leave, plus access to workplace nurseries. Excellent pension scheme with a generous employer contribution through auto-enrolment. Travel and shopping discounts at over 2,000 local and national retailers. Relocation support for eligible new starters. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you have any questions about this vacancy or the application process, please email Estates Division on EDR@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference BA49676 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information - Facilities Assistant Key information Department/location Estates Division (Facilities) Salary £27,319-£31,236 Reference BA49676 Category Assistant staff Date published 16 September 2026 Closing date 30 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/fly-laboratory-technician-part-time-fixed-term-pc49756": {
      "title": "Fly Laboratory Technician (Part Time, Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Fly Laboratory Technician (Part Time, Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Fly Laboratory Technician (Part Time, Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Fly Laboratory Technician (Part Time, Fixed Term) Applications are invited for a part-time Fly Laboratory Technician position in the Fly Facility at the Department of Genetics ( www.flyfacility.gen.cam.ac.uk ). The facility provides support for Drosophila (fruit fly) research and teaching both in Cambridge and beyond. The Fly Laboratory Technician will play an important role in the delivery of the facility's services and in the general operation of the facility. The Fly Laboratory Technician's primary responsibility will be to maintain fly stocks as part of the facility's stock-keeping service. In addition, they will contribute to the daily running of the facility and provide support during staff absence, or periods of increased demand for the facility's services. This work may involve assisting with the facility's fly food service, proving support for its microinjection service, or preparing materials for Drosophila teaching. The successful candidate should be educated to GCSE level grade A-C /NVQ level 2 or equivalent in a science subject/ equivalent level of practical experience. They should also have experience in an academic or commercial laboratory; basic IT skills, good communication, interpersonal and organizational skills and as well as having a methodical approach to problem solving. Previous experience of working with Drosophila is desirable, but not essential, as training will be provided. The position is offered on a part-time basis of 18.25 hours per week (0.5FTE) Monday to Friday and is fixed term for an initial period of 12 months in the first instance. We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for flexible working arrangements. The exact working pattern and working times will be agreed with the successful candidate. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. For informal enquiries about the role, please contact Dr Simon Collier, Fly Facility Manager psc38@cam.ac.uk Please quote reference PC49756 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Genetics Salary £25,249-£27,319 pro rata Reference PC49756 Category Assistant staff Date published 16 September 2026 Closing date 29 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/founding-dean-of-the-rokos-school-of-government-and-rokos-professorship-of-government-50944": {
      "title": "Founding Dean of the Rokos School of Government and Rokos Professorship of Government",
      "description": "Founding Dean of the Rokos School of Government and Rokos Professorship of Government | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Founding Dean of the Rokos School of Government and Rokos Professorship of Government Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Founding Dean of the Rokos School of Government and Rokos Professorship of Government The University of Cambridge is seeking an exceptional academic leader to become the founding Dean of the Rokos School of Government, alongside appointment to the Rokos Professorship of Government. This is a once-in-a-generation opportunity to shape a major new academic institution from its inception. At a time of profound change in government and public leadership, the School will bring together outstanding scholars and practitioners from across disciplines to address some of the most complex challenges facing governments and societies around the world. The founding Dean will shape the School's academic vision, culture and international reputation. They will build an outstanding interdisciplinary academic community, determine its research and educational priorities, and develop relationships across Cambridge and with governments, policymakers, international organisations, civil society, business and philanthropy. Central to the School's identity will be intellectual openness and independence. It will provide a space in which different and sometimes conflicting perspectives can be examined with rigour. The Dean will require the intellectual authority, independence and judgement to uphold the highest academic standards while enabling serious debate around complex and contested questions. We are seeking an individual of outstanding academic distinction and international standing. Candidates may come from a wide range of disciplinary backgrounds. Alongside an exceptional record of scholarship and research impact, they will bring the imagination and ambition to build something new, the ability to attract and inspire outstanding people, and the judgement and resilience to lead in a complex and highly visible environment. The successful candidate will be an influential and persuasive communicator, able to engage credibly with academic, governmental and wider public audiences. Experience of government, public policy, international affairs, diplomacy, industry or other relevant external environments would be welcomed, but no single professional or disciplinary background is prescribed. A PhD or equivalent postgraduate qualification is essential. Above all, this is an opportunity to define what a new School of Government at Cambridge can become: the people it will bring together, the questions it will ask and the ideas it will challenge. It is an opportunity to build an institution capable of making a lasting contribution to government and public leadership around the world. The Deanship is a five-year fixed-term appointment with the possibility of renewal. The successful candidate will also hold the Rokos Professorship of Government, which may be held to retiring age. The appointment is anticipated to commence in October 2027. The role will be based in Cambridge, and remuneration will reflect the credentials and seniority of the successful candidate. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Applications, including a letter, CV, research statement, publication list, and three referees, must be submitted online by Monday 26 October 2026. For queries about the application process please contact Human Resources at professorships@admin.cam.ac.uk . Informal queries should be directed to Professor Nigel Peake, Convenor of the Board: np100@cam.ac.uk ; and Dr Elisabeth Kendall, Mistress of Girton College: mistress.office@girton.cam.ac.uk . Please quote reference 50944 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Rokos School of Government Salary Reference 50944 Category Professorships and Directorships Date published 16 September 2026 Closing date 26 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/in-vivo-support-technician-animal-technician-rg51095": {
      "title": "In Vivo Support Technician (Animal Technician)",
      "description": "In Vivo Support Technician (Animal Technician) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs In Vivo Support Technician (Animal Technician) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs In Vivo Support Technician (Animal Technician) The Blouet Lab at the Institute of Metabolic Science, University of Cambridge is looking for an animal technician to support in vivo work in mouse models. Research in the Blouet group investigates the mechanisms through which the brain senses food and controls appetite and body weight. We use various surgical models to deliver drugs into the brain or specific tissues (gut), or sample blood in awake mice.\nWe combine these approaches with physiological assessments to measure weight gain, food intake, feeding behaviour, metabolism and glucose homeostasis. We are looking for a candidate who shows care and empathy towards animals under their charge, embraces the 3R's ethos, and is familiar with the legislative framework of the home office. The role holder will work alongside the Blouet team, checking health and wellbeing of the animals, and supporting our in vivo experiments by performing experimental procedures, collecting and processing animal samples, writing Standard Operating Procedures (SOP) and liaising between the Blouet team and the animal facility. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available until 31 March 2029 in the first instance. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure (criminal records check) check, a health assessment and a security check. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role on the online application form. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference RG51095 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particular Key information Department/location Department of Clinical Biochemistry Salary £25,249-£27,319 Reference RG51095 Category Assistant staff Date published 16 September 2026 Closing date 14 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
