    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-assistant-part-time-fixed-term-lj51070": {
      "title": "Research Assistant (Part Time, Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Assistant (Part Time, Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant (Part Time, Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant (Part Time, Fixed Term) The Department of Materials Science is looking for an enthusiastic part-time Research Assistant to work in Professor Judith Driscoll's research group - https://driscoll.msm.cam.ac.uk/ , for a fixed term 1 year contract, with the possibility of extension. The roleholder will act as a member of a research team carrying out research under the direction of the group leads to include data collection, analysis and some interpretation, using vacuum systems and inorganic chemicals. The roleholder will primarily work within the groups research lab assisting the group leads and the lab manager on experimental issues. Duties will include:\n-Liaison with the department's safety team on any safety matters (in collaboration with the lab manager)\n-Assisting the lab manager and group leads with relevant research/experiments.\n-Checking on working order of equipment and notifying group leads and lab manager of any problems\n-Leading on safety forms (e.g. COSHH, Risk Assessments) and equipment operation procedures.\n-Arranging weekly lab safety meetings.\n-Ensuring a correct chemical disposal process is followed.\n-Assisting on new equipment procurement, commissioning, placement and installation.\n-Assisting with relevant administrative duties at the direction of lab members. This role would be ideal for candidates interested in research, lab work and lab safety matters. Excellent communication and organisational skills are required, alongside a proactive, helpful approach to resolving issues within the lab. The successful candidate will be highly self-motivated, with effective prioritisation skills and the ability to deal with competing demands. Please refer to the linked Further Particulars for the full Job Description and Person Specification. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 1 years in the first instance. This role will be onsite, at the Department, for 2-3 days per week. Specific days and pattern can be discussed at interview. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. We encourage early applications, as the vacancy may close before the advertised deadline if a sufficient number of suitable applications are received. Documents in support of applications should include a CV and research statement. Informal enquiries are welcome and should be directed to Silvia Purro, sap200@cam.ac.uk Please quote reference LJ51070 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Materials Science and Metallurgy Salary £33,002-£35,608 pro rata Reference LJ51070 Category Research Date published 16 September 2026 Closing date 7 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/senior-bioinformatician-fixed-term-sw51093": {
      "title": "Senior Bioinformatician (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Senior Bioinformatician (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Senior Bioinformatician (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Senior Bioinformatician (Fixed Term) Join us at the world-leading CRUK Cambridge Institute. We're a unique department of the University of Cambridge, core funded by Cancer Research UK's charitable activities, and we're eagerly searching for a Senior Bioinformatician to join the Mair Group and lead computational analysis of metabolic heterogeneity in glioblastoma. The project will build on established and newly generated datasets to understand how tumour metabolism varies between and within patients, how metabolic state shapes tumour phenotype and heterogeneity, and how these processes influence response to therapy. A central focus will be the development and application of metabolic flux analysis paradigms, integrating isotope-tracing and mass-spectrometry-derived measurements with genomic, transcriptomic, epigenomic, single-cell, spatial and phenotypic data. The post-holder will use these data to identify metabolic programmes associated with distinct tumour states and therapeutic vulnerabilities, and to determine how metabolism changes during tumour evolution and treatment. This Grade 8 role will provide senior computational leadership for the programme. The post-holder will develop robust and reproducible analytical frameworks for metabolic and multi-omic data, integrate deeply characterised metabolic datasets with larger existing cohorts, and explore machine-learning and deep-learning approaches that can infer metabolic state, explain biological heterogeneity and predict treatment response. The role offers considerable scope to develop new computational methodology at the interface of cancer metabolism, systems biology and data science. Key responsibilities Working with Dr Mair, experimental cancer metabolism researchers, computational collaborators and clinical scientists, your key responsibilities will be to: Lead the design, implementation and validation of reproducible computational pipelines for metabolic profiling, isotope-tracing and metabolic flux analysis in glioblastoma. Develop quantitative approaches to link metabolic flux and metabolomic measurements to tumour phenotype, cellular state, intratumoural heterogeneity and response to therapy. Integrate metabolic datasets with genomics, transcriptomics, epigenomics, single-cell and spatial datasets, and where appropriate imaging, pathology and longitudinal clinical information. Apply statistical, machine-learning and deep-learning methods to identify metabolic programmes, infer metabolic state from larger datasets and develop predictive models of tumour behaviour and treatment response. Develop rigorous quality-control, normalisation, harmonisation and benchmarking frameworks across metabolic platforms, cohorts, experimental systems and timepoints. Work closely with experimental scientists to translate biological questions into computational analyses and to design experiments that maximise the interpretability of metabolic flux and multi-omic data. Lead computational analyses for high-quality publications, presentations and grant applications, and communicate complex quantitative concepts to multidisciplinary biological and clinical audiences. Provide day-to-day technical leadership and mentoring for junior computational researchers and students, and contribute to the longer-term development of the Mair Group computational metabolism programme. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 3 years in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and a CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. The closing date for applications is: 30th September 2026 The interview date for the role is: To be confirmed Please quote reference SW51093 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Cancer Research UK Cambridge Institute Salary £42,254-£56,535 Reference SW51093 Category Academic-related Date published 16 September 2026 Closing date 30 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/senior-operations-assistant-west-hub-ba51090": {
      "title": "Senior Operations Assistant",
      "description": "Senior Operations Assistant - West Hub | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Senior Operations Assistant - West Hub Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Senior Operations Assistant - West Hub The Senior Operations Assistant works closely with the Operations Supervisor and West Hub Operations Assistants to deliver an exceptional experience for all users of the West Hub. The role holder manages facilities management functions that support Operations, Catering and Library teams, supervising Operations Assistants and overseeing contractor activity on site. Acting as a daily point of leadership, the Senior Operations Assistant motivates and guides the team delivering face to face services, ensuring consistently high standards of customer service. They lead the team in lieu of the Operations Supervisor, supporting an inclusive, collaborative and forward thinking culture across the building. The role holder ensures Health and Safety compliance and acts as the fire evacuation contact point when the Operations Supervisor is not present. Working in partnership with the Operations Supervisor, they ensure daily duties, event set ups and the information desk service run smoothly, while supporting the development of operational processes and maintaining best practice in line with Estates Division Health & Safety Management. Key Responsibilities: Manage facilities management functions, ensuring cleaning, servicing of bookable and public spaces, minor maintenance and security services are delivered efficiently. Support daily building operations and contribute to developing operational procedures aligned with Estates KPIs and strategic goals. Process event requests and work with the Operations Supervisor and Event Coordinator to ensure smooth delivery of bookings. Plan resources for event set ups, monitor booking frequency, identify challenges and provide detailed end of day reports, escalating concerns as required. Ensure Health & Safety compliance and manage contractors on site, providing feedback to the Operations Manager. Role model exceptional customer service and support an open, inclusive and collaborative environment. About You: Experience working in a multi use venue, ideally with public access. Events management experience and experience in operational roles with varied priorities. Ability to manage competing priorities across diverse user groups and internal departments. Experience reporting and managing quantitative data, event contracting and finance processes. Experience working with contractors and internal service providers. Strong operational knowledge including opening/closing procedures, emergency processes and daily reporting. Experience in event delivery and planning, with marketing skills such as content creation, website updates and social media awareness. Educated to A Level/NVQ Level 3 or equivalent practical experience. First Aid, Fire Warden, COSHH trained or willing to undertake training. Inclusive, diplomatic and user focused, with strong communication and teamworking skills. Practical, solutions focused, self motivated and adaptable. Willingness to work shift patterns, including some unsociable hours and weekends. Benefits: Competitive pay, annual cost of living increases, generous leave, flexible working, family friendly policies, excellent pension, retail discounts and relocation support for eligible new starters Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you have any questions about this vacancy or the application process, please email Estates Division on EDR@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference BA51090 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information - Senior Operations Assistant Key information Department/location Estates Division Salary £27,319-£31,236 Reference BA51090 Category Assistant staff Date published 16 September 2026 Closing date 30 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/academic-support-coordinator-part-time-fixed-term-sj50652": {
      "title": "Academic Support Coordinator (Part Time, Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Academic Support Coordinator (Part Time, Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Academic Support Coordinator (Part Time, Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Academic Support Coordinator (Part Time, Fixed Term) IMS Epidemiology is seeking to appoint an Academic Support Coordinator to provide administrative, organisational and research support to a Professor with a long-term illness. The postholder will help the Professor manage communications, documents, information, meetings and workflows, ensuring the efficient and effective management of their energy and time to support the sustainable delivery of their academic, research, supervisory and strategic responsibilities. IMS Epidemiology is a Division within the University of Cambridge School of Clinical Medicine, situated within the Institute of Metabolic Science on the Cambridge Biomedical Campus. Its mission is to inform, develop and evaluate approaches to promote health and wellbeing through the joint strategies of precision prevention and population health improvement. This position is based in the Population Health Interventions group ( https://www.epi.ims.cam.ac.uk/research/research-areas/population-health-interventions/ ), an interdisciplinary team combining epidemiological, public health and social science approaches to understanding how environmental and policy changes can improve diet, physical activity and population health. The successful candidate will provide practical coordination and support across a varied portfolio of academic and professional activities. This will include coordinating meetings and schedules; planning and managing workflows; preparing agendas, minutes, briefing notes and follow-up actions; triaging and batching correspondence; organising travel and related arrangements; maintaining filing and document management systems; supporting expenditure, purchasing and reimbursement processes; and coordinating, preparing and formatting documents and presentation materials for publications, grant applications, lectures, briefings and other professional outputs. The role will also involve preliminary research using a range of information sources, including searching for and collating scientific literature and related material. The postholder will be expected to work confidently with academic papers, committee papers, grant-related documents, correspondence and presentation materials, helping to maintain continuity across complex workflows. We are looking for someone with previous high-level experience of executive assistance, personal assistance, project coordination or research coordination, ideally in higher education or another research setting. You will be comfortable working both independently and closely with the Professor, and able to demonstrate excellent organisational, planning, communication and interpersonal skills combined with discretion, flexibility and sound judgement. This is an enabling support role in which all academic, research, supervisory and strategic responsibilities will remain with the Professor. It would suit someone who has an understanding of public health research and takes satisfaction in bringing structure, continuity and follow-through to complex academic activity in a way that balances the need for support with respecting professional autonomy. This is a Grade 5 post funded for 20 hours per week through the UK Government's Access to Work scheme ( https://www.gov.uk/access-to-work ). The post is available for twelve months in the first instance, with potential for extension to three years in total subject to the continuing availability of Access to Work funding. Prospective applicants who would like to understand more about the nature of the role and their potential suitability are encouraged to make a pre-application enquiry to Professor David Ogilvie ( david.ogilvie@ims.cam.ac.uk ) to discuss this further. The closing date for applications is Sunday 4 October 2026 and interviews will be held on Monday 12 October. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 12 months in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role on the online application form. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference SJ50652 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Clinical Biochemistry (Institute of Metabolic Science Epidemiology) Salary £31,236-£35,608 pro rata Reference SJ50652 Category Assistant staff Date published 17 September 2026 Closing date 4 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
