      "title": "Research Assistant / Research Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Assistant / Research Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant / Research Associate (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant / Research Associate (Fixed Term) Applications are invited for a Research Assistant / Associate to lead a project at the intersection of human stem cell biology, disease modelling, functional genomics, and translational neuroscience. The successful candidate will develop and utilise cutting-edge patient stem cell-derived models of progressive MS to investigate the molecular mechanisms driving maladaptive glial responses, apply functional genomic approaches to identify and validate novel disease-modifying targets, and advance the translation of these discoveries towards effective therapies. About the CCMR The UK MS Society Cambridge Centre for Myelin Repair (CCMR) is one of the MS Society's two national Centres of Excellence. Established in 2005, it has just been renewed for a fifth five-year term (CCMR5) with the mission of accelerating the development of effective treatments for progressive MS, a condition for which no therapies currently exist to halt disability progression. Led by Professors Káradóttir, Pluchino and Coles, CCMR5 brings together six laboratories across the Department of Clinical Neurosciences and the Cambridge Stem Cell Institute, with research fellows, postdocs, clinical scientists and PhD students working across three integrated Themes: identifying the determinants of remyelination and progression (#1), validating them in next-generation human models (#2), and biomarkers of remyelination in people with MS (#3). This post sits in Theme #2, led by Professor Pluchino, and works closely with Theme #1. You will join an active community with an annual symposium, structured postdoctoral mentoring (all four CCMR4-funded postdocs went on to secure personal fellowships) and a wide international collaborator network. About You You will hold a PhD in cell biology, biochemistry, molecular biology, or a closely related field, with a strong background in cellular engineering and gene editing (including CRISPR-Cas9 and CRISPR-based screens) and demonstrated experience culturing human stem cells (iPSCs). You will have excellent data analysis and communication skills, a track record of scientific output, and the ability to work both independently and within a collaborative, multidisciplinary team. Experience with single-cell omics, bioinformatics, epigenomics or cell metabolism is desirable. Full criteria are set out in the Further Particulars. This is a Grade 7 Research Associate post. Candidates who have submitted their PhD but not yet been awarded it may be appointed initially as a Research Assistant (Grade 5) and uplifted to Research Associate once the PhD is awarded. What We Offer Pioneering Research The Pluchino laboratory is internationally recognised for its work on cell metabolism, stem cell biology, and neurodegeneration, with high-impact publications in Cell Stem Cell, Neuron, and Nature family journals. A Highly Collaborative Environment Close interactions with the Department of Clinical Neurosciences, the Cambridge Stem Cell Institute, the Clinical School and the UK Dementia Research Institute on the Cambridge Biomedical Campus. Career Development and Mentorship Dedicated supervision and regular one-to-one meetings with Professor Pluchino Opportunity to lead high-impact projects and publish in top-tier journals Access to the University of Cambridge's extensive researcher development programme Support to develop an independent research identity, including contributing to grant applications and presenting at national and international conferences Useful links: MS Society Cambridge Centre for Myelin Repair ( https://www.stemcells.cam.ac.uk/research/ms-society-cambridge-centre-myelin-repair ); Pluchino Laboratory ( www.pluchinolab.com ). Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a health assessment and a security check. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Closing Date: 11th October 2026 Interview Date: to be confirmed Please quote reference RR51113 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Clinical Neurosciences Salary £34,610 - £35,608 £37,694 - £46,049 Reference RR51113 Category Research Date published 17 September 2026 Closing date 11 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-assistantresearch-associate-fixed-term-nr51111": {
      "title": "Research Assistant/Research Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Assistant/Research Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant/Research Associate  (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant/Research Associate  (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 6 months in the first instance. Applications are invited for a Research Assistant or Associate to work on program summarisation.  We are interested in how benchmark applications can be summarised so that only a small fraction of them needs to be run for experimentation and evaluation of future computing systems.  The parts that are executed should be representative of the program as a whole.  The successful candidate will develop techniques for breaking an application up into suitably small parts, grouping similar code and identifying representative fragments. Research Group Our group consists of PostDocs and PhD students investigating performance, reliability and security in future many-core systems. The position is within the Computer Architecture Research Group at the University of Cambridge Computer Laboratory.  Collaboration with researchers at other universities and industry around the world is encouraged and there are strong links within the group with local companies. Candidates We seek candidates with a strong background in Computer Science with a particular interest in computer architectures and compilers.  It is essential to have experience developing program analysis tools and existing schemes for program summarisation.  Familiarity working in teams with robust engineering practices (version control, continuous integration, automated testing) would be useful.  The ideal candidate would have an awareness of the challenges and potential solutions to application profiling. Applicants should hold a PhD or at least be close to submitting their thesis.  Knowledge of a related area would be advantageous but not essential.  Candidates should provide evidence of relevant work, where possible, and must demonstrate a desire to perform internationally leading research and to publish in the top conferences in the area. Please provide a Curriculum Vitae (CV) and a short covering letter outlining your relevant past experience, drawing particular attention to relevant experience and published papers, and linking to one or more examples of code written (e.g. a GitHub handle).  If there are specific areas you would like to research, please indicate these in the letter. More Information Appointment at Research Associate level is dependent on having a PhD. Those who have submitted but not yet received their PhD will be appointed at Research Assistant level, which will be amended to Research Associate once the PhD has been awarded. The funds are available for a start date of the 1st October 2026 but some flexibility on this can be arranged. Further details may be obtained from Prof. Timothy Jones timothy.jones@cl.cam.ac.uk In addition to the base salary stated above, the successful candidate for this post will receive an additional 2.5% supplement to their pay. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Questions about the post and the recruitment process may be addressed to the HR Team at hr-admin@cst.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference NR51111 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Computer Science and Technology Salary £33,002-£46,049 Reference NR51111 Category Research Date published 17 September 2026 Closing date 27 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-assistantresearch-associate-fixed-term-jc51118": {
      "title": "Research Assistant/Research Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Assistant/Research Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant/Research Associate (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant/Research Associate (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 24 months in the first instance. The McDonald Institute for Archaeological Research invites applications for a full-time, fixed-term Research Associate in Prehistoric Cycladic Archaeometallurgy, funded by the Leventis Foundation and the McDonald Institute. The postholder will (i) complete the scientific analysis and interpretation of metallic and metallurgical material from excavations at Ayia Irini on the Cycladic island of Kea, Greece, and (ii) write up and fully publish these results. They will work under the co-supervision at the McDonald Institute of Professor Cyprian Broodbank, Disney Professor of Archaeology and Institute Director, with expertise in Cycladic archaeology, and Professor Marcos Martinón-Torres, Pitt-Rivers Professor of Archaeological Science. External supervision will be provided by Professor Natalie Abell (University of Michigan; Director of publications at Ayia Irini), and Dr Evangelia Kiriatzi (Director, Fitch Laboratory of Archaeological Science, British School at Athens). The appointee will join a thriving community of interdisciplinary researchers providing ample opportunities for collaboration, training and publication. We seek a highly organised, self-driven individual with competence in both Cycladic Prehistory and archaeometallurgy. Appointment at Research Associate is dependent on having a relevant PhD by the start of the appointment, including applicants who have submitted their thesis but not yet received their PhD at the time of interview (in which case appointment will initially be made as Research Assistant (Grade 5) and amended to Research Associate (Grade 7) when the PhD is awarded). Please upload your CV, covering letter and a writing sample (maximum 5,000 words) as part of your application. Interviews: Week of 26th October 2026 or shortly thereafter Starting date: 1st December 2026 or as soon as possible thereafter We particularly encourage women and /or candidates from a Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic background to apply for this vacancy as they are currently under-represented at this level within our University. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be directed to Professor Cyprian Broodbank ( cb122@cam.ac.uk ) or Professor Marcos Martinón-Torres ( m.martinon-torres@arch.cam.ac.uk ). If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact the HSPS HR team: hr@hsps.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference JC51118 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information JC51118 Research Assistant Associate in Prehistoric Cycladic Archaeometallurgy FPs Key information Department/location McDonald Institute for Archaeological Research Salary £34,610 - £35,608 £37,694 - £46,049 Reference JC51118 Category Research Date published 17 September 2026 Closing date 23 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/senior-building-services-technician-ba51092": {
      "title": "Senior Building Services Technician",
      "description": "Senior Building Services Technician | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Senior Building Services Technician Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Senior Building Services Technician The Role: Senior Building Services Technician, Cavendish Estate The Estates Division team based on the Cavendish Estate are looking for an enthusiastic person to join their team. The Cavendish Estate is predominantly based on the West Cambridge site and is where the Department of Physics operate from. The Cavendish Estate consists of multiple buildings, including the new Ray Dolby Centre. Your Responsibilities This role will see you be responsible for maintenance and small project works on the Cavendish Estate. You will be a vital part in the on-site maintenance/project team, ensuring the Cavendish Estate buildings are suitably maintained. You will have the responsibility for looking after your own works packages, as well as co-ordinating with other members of your team. In this role you will provide support for making sure visiting contractors are appropriately booked in and inducted. As part of this role, you will carry out small project tasks and support with mechanical duties. You will work collaboratively with the Department of Physics and other Estate Division employees. Carry out reactive repairs, breakdown maintenance and planned preventative maintenance (PPM) on mechanical and associated building services plant and equipment. Operate and monitor plant room equipment, ensuring systems are maintained safely, efficiently and in accordance with operational procedures. Perform routine safety inspections and maintain accurate records of statutory checks, inspections and maintenance activities. Diagnose faults and undertake first-line repairs to mechanical engineering plant and equipment, escalating specialist issues where required. Conduct inspections of control panels, pumps, extract systems, air handling units (AHUs) and other building engineering infrastructure. Prioritise maintenance activities and undertake technical investigations to identify root causes of building services issues. Liaise with Estates Division services and external contractors to arrange servicing, repairs and remedial works as required. Coordinate and administer Permit to Work systems, including issuing, monitoring and closing permits for controlled work areas. Provide technical guidance and operational advice to building occupants and contractors regarding maintenance and engineering activities. Support refurbishment and workplace improvement projects, including decorating, installation of shelving, benches, cupboards and other laboratory or office fittings. Carry out minor alterations to associated building fabric and services tasks, where competent and authorised to do so. Work collaboratively with the Building Services Manager and wider on-site Operations team to maintain a safe, compliant and customer-focused environment across the Cavendish Estate. About you: In order to succeed in this role, you will join us with benefits of working at the University of Cambridge include: Competitive rates of pay with automatic service-related pay progression and annual cost of living increases; Generous annual leave allowance Flexible and hybrid working opportunities; Generous maternity, adoption and shared parental leave entitlement and other family friendly schemes (e.g. workplace nurseries) An auto-enrolment pension scheme, with a generous employer contribution; Travel benefits and retail discounts at over 2,000 local and national stores; Schemes to support with relocation Interviews will be held week commencing 12th October 2026 Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you have any questions about this vacancy or the application process, please email Estates Division on EDR@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference BA51092 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information - Senior Services Building Technician Key information Department/location Estates Division (Facilities) Salary £31,236-£35,608 Reference BA51092 Category Assistant staff Date published 16 September 2026 Closing date 4 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/senior-research-associate-part-time-fixed-term-rd51116": {
      "title": "Senior Research Associate (Part Time, Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Senior Research Associate (Part Time, Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Senior Research Associate (Part Time, Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Senior Research Associate (Part Time, Fixed Term) This is an exciting opportunity for an experienced, ambitious, talented and highly motivated researcher to join our team within the Precision Breast Cancer Institute (PBCI), led by Professor Jean Abraham, and make a real impact in the fight against breast cancer. Breast cancers comprise complex biological processes that function in compound microenvironments of malignant tumour cells. As such, analysing digital pathology images can offer insight into underlying cancer mechanisms, leading to more accurate diagnosis, prognosis, and treatment planning for cancer patients. The successful candidate will play a pivotal role in revolutionising patient care through the design, development, and application of novel computer vision pipelines analyse digital pathology images, primarily derived from large clinical trials and studies. The role will also involve working closely with renowned scientists, liaising with project teams and external science users, contributing to research publications, and collaborating with clinical experts to optimise the interpretation and use of this data. At PBCI, we believe in fostering a collaborative and supportive work environment. We value teamwork, respect, and open communication, creating a space where ideas thrive and individuals flourish. Together, we will support and inspire one another as we work towards our shared goal of improving breast cancer treatments and outcomes. Together, we will shape the future of breast cancer research and drive impactful and rapid change. The successful candidate will deliver digital pathology projects across the PBCI and collaborators within the University and Cambridge University Hospital. A such, the ability to work as part of a multi-disciplinary team and to be able to communicate to a range of audiences is required. Join us in our mission to develop clinically vision pipelines, drive high-impact publications, and pave the way for personalised breast cancer treatments. We welcome the opportunity to have a conversation and answer any questions you may have about the position and the PBCI. Please reach out to Prof Jean Abraham ( ja344@medschl.cam.ac.uk ) and Dr Joanna Worley ( jrw86@cam.ac.uk )  to learn more about this exciting opportunity. Closing date: 30 September 2026 (23:59PM UK) Interview Date: Week of the 5 October 202. Starting Date: From 19th October 2026 or as early as possible thereafter. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available until 31 March 2028 in the first instance. This appointment requires a Research Passport application. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and a CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference RD51116 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Oncology Salary £42,254-£56,535 pro rata Reference RD51116 Category Research Date published 17 September 2026 Closing date 30 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
